Although economy and society have greatly developed at the present stage, health problem is still a big challenge that human beings encounter for which two factors including environment issues and delayed disease detection should take the main responsibility. On one hand, irregular and excessive production activities lead to inestimable global pollutions such as water, air and soil contamination since the 21th century [1–4]. These pollutions have exerted bad effects on people's normal life and caused threats to human bodies [5–8]. For example, carcinogen metals including nickel, cadmium and chromium in water generally induce lung and kidney cancers [8]. On the other hand, unhealthy diet and unreasonable lifestyle also give rise to serious diseases [9,10], especially the increase in the incidence of many diseases such as diabetes mellitus, Alzheimer's disease and cancers [9]. In order to cope with so many life-threatening diseases, researchers have put forward two solutions that
appear to be the good choices, including environmental pretreatment and early diagnosis of diseases.
For environmental pre-treatment, detecting and removing hazardous pollutants are the top priority. Generally speaking, there are diverse hazardous pollutants, including plasticizers, pesticides, pharmaceutical and personal care products (PPCPs), organic dyes and heavy metals. These pollutants are constantly accumulated in the external environment and somehow enter human body, resulting in irreversible damage. Highly efficient techniques to simultaneously detect and remove these contaminants are of great significance for improving the survival environment and living quality [11,12]. For early diagnosis of diseases, seeking reliable biomarkers apparently becomes urgent. Proteins are material bases of life and take part in numerous life activities, the expression level of which can correspondingly reflect the physiological and pathological status of human body [13]. In recent years, a large number of reports have indicated the great potential of proteins/peptides as biomarkers [14–17]. However, human samples such as serum, saliva and urine are quite dynamic and complex. Moreover, these promising protein/peptide biomarkers are usually in extremely low abundance. For instance, dynamic range of protein levels in plasma reaches 12 orders of magnitude. While 22 plasma proteins already occupy \(99\%\) of the total protein mass [18]. That's to say the potential biomarkers are more likely to appear in the remaining \(1\%\) , indicating the difficulty of seeking specific proteins/peptides. Hence effective separation of desired proteins/peptides from complex samples is the premise of biomarker screening.
Owing to splendid features of rigid structure, large specific surface area, thermal and chemical stability, numerous active sites and easy functionalization, nanomaterials have been widely recognized in the sample pre-treatment process [19–25]. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), firstly created by Yaghi et al. [26], are a typical class of organic-inorganic porous nanomaterials via self-assembly coordination between metal ions/clusters and organic ligands. In the past two decades, in addition to room-temperature synthesis and conventional electric heating method, more and more advanced techniques including microwave-assisted heating, electrochemistry, mechanochemistry and sonochemistry have been explored to obtain MOFs with different morphologies and properties. High-throughput methods based on automated setups have also been reported to investigate the effect of single reaction parameter on final products and optimize synthesis conditions. Because of the significant progress in synthesis, MOFs have been widely developed and been a hot topic [27]. Apart from the common characteristics of other porous nanomaterials, the most outstanding advantages of MOFs are structure diversity and extensive functionalities, which derive from multiple combination of metal centers and organic ligands [28]. Based on
On the whole, the MOF-based sample pre-treatment technique universally holds distinguished advantages,
including outstanding adsorption efficiency, considerable reproducibility, simple design, easy operation, minimal energy consumption, and remarkable cost-effectiveness, which contributes a great deal to progress in proteomics and environmental research. In light of remarkable contributions of MOFs to the above two important fields, this review provides a summary of representative research about MOFs as advanced adsorbent materials for separation and analysis of complex samples in the last 5 years. Specifically, novel design and synthesis of functional MOFs and their applications in glycosylation/phosphorylation analysis and enzyme immobilization are firstly discussed. Then MOF-based adsorbents for effective detection and removal of various contaminations, including plasticizers, pesticides and water pollutants (PPCPs, organic dyes and heavy metals), are briefly introduced. In the end, the vital role of MOFs in sample pre-treatment field is appreciated and some challenges are also put forward.
## 2 MOFs in proteomics research
Post-translational modification (PTM) is an extremely important research domain, which is closely related to basic mechanisms concerning the onset and progression of human diseases [51,52]. As indicated above, MOFs with remarkable properties can serve as pre-treatment adsorbents for glycopeptide/phosphopeptide separation. In addition to performing separation duties by their own ligands or metal centers, MOFs are normally combined with other substrates which can provide auxiliary functionalities. For instance, the well-known magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) particles can offer fast separation ability, thereby reducing time consumption and sample loss [53]. Besides, MOF-derived carbon materials often reserve preeminent characteristics of MOFs that are suitable for glycan separation [46]. Notably, the excellent physical properties such as structural diversity, large specific surface area and high porosity lead MOFs to be a good support for carrying enzyme molecules, which improve enzyme stability and guarantee enzyme activity [54]. In this section, applications of MOFs in glycopeptide enrichment, phosphopeptide enrichment, glycan enrichment and enzyme immobilization will be emphatically introduced.
## 2.1 Glycopeptide enrichment
As one of the most common PTMs, glycosylation occurs on more than half of proteins in human bodies by the combination of varied glycans and specific amino acid residues [55–57]. Glycosylation has been reported to mediate many biological processes including protein folding, receptor recognition, and cell protection, thus affecting the life health [58]. Abnormal glycosylation usually brings diseases and
As one of the most common PTMs, glycosylation occurs on more than half of proteins in human bodies by the combination of varied glycans and specific amino acid residues [55–57]. Glycosylation has been reported to mediate many biological processes including protein folding, receptor recognition, and cell protection, thus affecting the life health [58]. Abnormal glycosylation usually brings diseases and even cancers in severe cases where glycosylation alternations in cell surface accelerate the invasive behavior of tumor cells [59]. Owing to such great significance, in the past ten years continuous efforts have been made for glycosylation research to seek potential biomarkers [60]. However, the abundance and ionization efficiency of glycopeptides are relatively low and the co-existence of interferents is inevitable, which leads to signal suppression. So one key point for glycosylation research lies in glycopeptide enrichment [61]. Inspired by the exclusive glycan units of glycopeptides, several methods including HILIC, boronate affinity chromatography, hydrazine chemistry, and lectin affinity chromatography have been widely explored [62]. Thereinto, HILIC technique, which depends on the formation of hydration layer around hydrophilic molecules to provide affinity towards glycan units through hydrogen-bond effect, is favored by the unbiased recognition ability and high coverage towards glycopeptides [63]. As a consequence, hydrophilic MOFs are widely explored in glycoproteomics.
Generally, there are two ways to endow MOFs with great hydrophilicity. Either way, the resultant hydrophilic MOFs can perform well for glycopeptide enrichment. One is to choose excellently hydrophilic organic ligands to construct MOFs. Obviously, for this method, MOFs alone play the role of adsorbents through the interaction between the hydrophilic organic ligands and glycopeptides. Taking Zr-MOFs for example, carboxyl-functionalized Zr-MOFs (denoted as UiO-66-COOH) were prepared by using isophthalic acid to replace a portion of terephthalic acid [64]. With the ligand substitution, free carboxyl groups were introduced to bring Zr-MOFs good hydrophilicity, leading to great enrichment performance towards glycopeptides in human serum. Compared with pure UiO-66, all UiO-66-COOH materials with different contents of free carboxyl groups revealed better enrichment performance. Especially, UiO-66-COOH(20) with only 20% isophthalic acid ligand performed best, which should be attributed to the synergistic effect of great hydrophilicity and crystal quality that accelerated the host-guest interaction between MOFs and glycopeptides. In addition to high sensitivity (0.5 fmol μL−1 of HRP digest), repeatability (test for five times) and stability (test for two months), a total of 255 N-glycopeptides corresponding to 93 different glycoproteins were identified in 2 μL human serum by UiO-66-COOH(20). Moreover, the ligand of UiO-66 can also be replaced by other hydrophilic ligands such as 2-sulfoterephthalic acid. In Xie's work [65], they utilized 2-sulfoterephthalic acid to prepare new UiO-66 on magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanoparticles, and the final product was denoted as \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{Zr}-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\). Notably, the magnetic core can reduce time consumption to a certain extent compared with UiO-66-COOH(20) which employed centrifugation separation. Finally, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{Zr}-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) revealed high sensitivity (0.1 fmol μL−1 for HRP digest), great selectivity (HRP
digest/BSA digest=1/100, \(m/m\)), and identified 177 \(N\)-glycopeptides from 85 different glycoproteins in \(2~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) human serum. Evidently, this convenient adjustability of organic ligands for changing properties is a unique advantage of MOFs.
Hydrophilic Ti-MOFs are also applicable in glycopeptide enrichment. Yang et al. [66] constructed the two-dimensional (2D) Ti-MOF nanosheets by employing hydrophilic 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid. The uniform sheet structure offered large specific area and increased hydrophilic interaction sites. Based on these, 2D Ti-MOF nanosheets exhibited 0.1 fmol \(\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) of high sensitivity using HRP digest as test sample. The porous structure endowed the 2D Ti-MOF nanosheets with the ability to allow small-size glycopeptide molecules to enter porous channels, while exclude large-size protein molecules outside. By virtue of the abundant hydrophilic interaction sites and porous structure, the great size-exclusion effect of 2D Ti-MOF nanosheets was evaluated as 1/800/800 (HRP digest/HRP/BSA, \(n/n/n\)) [66]. Besides, Du's group [67] synthesized an amino-functionalized Ti-MOF, namely \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125(Ti), using 2-aminoterephthalic acid ligand, followed by packing it in pipette tip for endogenous glycopeptide capture. Owing to the outstanding selectivity (HRP digest/BSA digest=1/1,000, \(m/m\)) and size-exclusion effect (HRP digest/OVA/IgG=1/350/350, \(m/m/m\)), \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125(Ti) identified 64 endogenous \(N\)-glycopeptides from human saliva.
Additionally, magnetic Mg-MOFs (denoted as \(\mathrm{MagG}@\mathrm{Mg}\)-MOFs) were created by utilizing magnetic graphene (MagG) as support for carrying hydrophilic Mg-MOFs as seen in Figure 1 [68]. The delocalized \(\pi{-}\pi\) electron structure of MagG increased the adsorption amount of hydrophilic Mg-MOF layer, and hydrophilic Mg-MOF layer
with micropores could selectively extract small-size glycopeptides. With the perfect combination of MagG and Mg-MOFs layer, \(\mathrm{MagG}@\mathrm{Mg}\)-MOFs displayed high sensitivity (0.1 fmol \(\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) HRP digest), loading capacity (150 mg g\(^{-1}\) for HRP digest), recovery (95.9% and 87.2% for two deglycosylated peptides) and size-exclusion effect (HRP digest/HRP/BSA=1/500/500, \(m/m/m\)). Finally, 406 \(N\)-glycopeptides corresponding to 185 glycoproteins were identified by \(\mathrm{MagG}@\mathrm{Mg}\)-MOFs from human urine of bladder cancer patients, some of which were the potential biomarkers of bladder cancer that have been previously reported.
The other way to endow MOFs with great hydrophilicity is to furtherly functionalize as-synthesized MOFs with additional hydrophilic groups, namely post-synthetic modification. This strategy not only retains the original characteristics of MOFs but also regulates the surface hydrophilicity of MOFs in different manners, thus broadening the application of MOFs. Accordingly, MOFs with improved hydrophilicity through post-synthetic modification constantly spring up for advancing glycoproteomics research. Owing to the outstanding thermal, chemical stability and abundant amino groups on its surface, UiO-66-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) has been widely chosen as reliable substrate for further modification for constructing hydrophilic nanocomposites [69]. For instance, Saleem et al. [70] immobilized UiO-66-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) onto graphene oxide (GO), followed by the modification of 4-carboxy phenylboronic acid to obtain GO@UiO-66-PBA. Apart from great selectivity (IgG digest/BSA digest=1/200, \(\nu/\nu\)), good sensitivity (1 fmol \(\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) IgG digest), binding capacity (84 mg g\(^{-1}\) for HRP digest) and recovery (86.5% for HRP digest), GO@UiO-66-PBA identified 372 \(N\)-glycopeptides corresponding to 33 different glycoproteins from 1 \(\upmu\mathrm{L}\) serum digest. Hu et al. [71] chose glucose-6-phosphate (G6P) as
hydrophilic molecules to modify UiO-66-NH\(_2\) via the electrostatic and coordination interaction between phosphate groups of G6P and Zr centers in MOFs. The immobilization amount of G6P was optimized through Zeta potential measurement, and finally \(1.5\;\mathrm{mg\;mL}^{-1}\) of G6P precursor solution was selected. The final product was denoted as Mag ZrMOF@G6P-3, which was applied for subsequent performance test and urinary glycopeptide analysis. The result showed Zr-MOF@G6P-3 displayed high sensitivity (0.1 fmolL \(\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\)), great selectivity (HRP digest/BSA digest=1/200, \(m/m\)) and good binding capacity \((80\;\mathrm{mg\;g}^{-1}\) for HRP digest). And 123 and 111 \(N\)-glycopeptides corresponding to 78 and 76 glycoproteins were respectively identified in \(50\;\upmu\mathrm{L}\) urine of kidney cancer patients and healthy controls. Similarly, Peng et al. [72] modified fructose-1,6-diphosphate (FDP) on dual-metal-centered Zr-MOF (DZMOF) through electrostatic and coordination interaction between phosphate groups of FDP and Zr centers in MOFs. The most suitable concentration of FDP in the modification process was evaluated, and \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\) was decided for the construction of DZMOF-FDP\(_{10}\). Due to the modification of hydrophilic FDP, DZMOF-FDP\(_{10}\) displayed the excellent size-exclusion effect (IgG digest/BSA=1/1,000, \(n/n\)), sensitivity (0.1 fmolL \(\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) IgG digest) and selectivity (IgG digest/BSA digest=1/5,000, \(n/n\)). Finally, DZMOF-FDP\(_{10}\) successfully identified 380 endogenous \(N\)-glycopeptides including 180 specific glycosylation sites from \(5\;\upmu\mathrm{L}\) human plasma, among which mono-glycosylation dominated.
The above-mentioned designs have contributed a lot to glycopeptide analysis. However, most of post-synthetic modification processes are labor-intensive and time-consuming. Therefore, other modification processes with easy operation have been explored. To be specific, "Au–S"-based strategy attracts much attention. Briefly, chloroauric acid molecules are converted to uniform Au nanoparticles on the surface of amino groups of UiO-66-NH\(_2\) via in-situ reduction. Then various hydrophilic compounds containing sulfhydryl are anchored through Au–S bonds. Thanks to this convenient strategy, three different hydrophilic compounds including glutathione (GSH), mercaptosuccinic acid (MSA) and maltose were successively immobilized on UiO-66-NH\(_2\) recently [73–75]. Benefiting from the improved host-guest hydrophilic interaction through "Au–S"-based strategy, these three resultant MOFs exhibited better enrichment performance than pristine UiO-66-NH\(_2\). Moreover, all of them were applied to serum glycosylation analysis, and identified 273, 307 and \(113\;N\)-glycopeptides corresponding to 94, 96 and 46 glycoproteins from \(2\;\upmu\mathrm{L}\) healthy human serum, \(2\;\upmu\mathrm{L}\) serum of breast cancer patients and \(1\;\upmu\mathrm{L}\) healthy human serum, respectively.
Furthermore, MIL-101 was also utilized for modification with hydrophilic groups, such as poly(amidoamine) (PAMAM) and cysteine (Cys). The final products were denoted
as MIL-101(Cr)-NH\(_2\)\@PAMAM and MIL-101-NH\(_2\)\@Au-Cys, respectively [76,77]. As expected, the result demonstrated that both PAMAM and Cys enhanced the hydrophilicity of MOFs and endowed them with better enrichment performance compared with pristine MOFs. Furtherly, MIL-101(Cr)-NH\(_2\)\@PAMAM identified 92 \(N\)-glycopeptides corresponding to 46 glycoproteins from \(5\;\upmu\mathrm{L}\) human serum, and MIL-101-NH\(_2\)\@Au-Cys identified 1,069 \(N\)-glycopeptides from 684 glycoproteins in \(150\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\) HeLa cell lysate. In addition, Lan's group [78,79] explored post-synthetic modification of Ce-MOFs. Firstly, they modified Ce-MOFs with hyaluronic acid (HA) and glutamic acid (Glu), and the resulting products were denoted as Ce-MOF@HA and Ce-MOF@Glu, respectively [78]. Since Ce-MOF was stable in neutral polar aprotic solvents (loading buffer), whereas degradable in polar protic solvents (eluent), conventional elution step can be omitted. This reduced time and labor consumption and sample loss. Both of Ce-MOF@HA and Ce-MOF@Glu revealed great sensitivity (0.5 and 0.1 fmolL \(\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) IgG digest, respectively) and selectivity (IgG digest/BSA digest=1:1,000 and 1:500, \(n/n\), respectively). Furthermore, they identified 434 and 328 \(N\)-glycopeptides corresponding to 182 and 135 glycoproteins, respectively from \(2\;\upmu\mathrm{L}\) human serum. Later, Lan's group [79] constructed phytic acid (PA)-functionalized mesoporous Ce-MOF (denoted as Ce-MOF@PA). In their work, polyvinylpyrrolidone was introduced into MOF precursor solution for bonding with Ce ions, which sterically protected Ce–O bonds from hydrolysis. The obtained Ce-MOF was stable in aqueous solution. Besides, the subsequent addition of PA not only converted original micropore structure of Ce-MOF into mesopore structure, but also enhanced its surface hydrophilicity. Small-size glycopeptides could enter pore channels and interact with hydrophilic moieties on the inner surface. Based on this, Ce-MOF@PA displayed great size-exclusion effect (IgG digest/IgG/BSA=1/1,000/1,000, \(n/n/n\)). It also showed higher selectivity (IgG digest/BSA digest=1/500, \(n/n\)) and recovery \((93.7\%{\pm}6.4\%)\) compared with pristine Ce-MOF (1/200 of selectivity and \(69.5\%{\pm}13.4\%\) of recovery). Finally, a total of 422 glycopeptides assigned to 155 glycoproteins were identified from \(2\;\upmu\mathrm{L}\) human serum in three parallel replicates. Furthermore, aminophenylboronic acid-functionalized Zn-MOF composite (denoted as terpolymer@ZIF-8@BA) was designed for serum glycosylation research. And 318 \(N\)-glycopeptides were identified by liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry (LC-MS) [80]. This research also indicated the potential of ZIF series as substrate for further hydrophilic modification. The rough summary of HILIC-based MOFs for glycopeptide analysis is displayed in Table 1.
## 2.2 Phosphopeptide enrichment
Protein phosphorylation is another vital part of PTMs, which
mostly occurs on serine, threonine and tyrosine and acts as the most basic, universal and important mechanism in regulating protein functions and cellular metabolism [81,82]. To be specific, serine phosphorylation activates protein vitality by changing protein conformation, and tyrosine accelerates the interaction between phosphoproteins and others to form complexes for modulating cell signal transduction [83]. Since abnormal phosphorylation is also closely associated with many diseases [84,85], research intensity on phosphorylation is comparable to glycosylation. Meanwhile, the inevitable barriers for MS detection in phosphorylation researches also include low abundance, poor ionization efficacy and high dynamic range of phosphopeptides [86,87]. Therefore, many strategies, especially immobilized metal ion affinity chromatography (IMAC) and MOAC, have been developed [88,89]. The extensive applications of IMAC and MOAC are attributed to the robust bonding between metal ions/oxides and phosphate groups through electrostatic and coordination interaction.
Interestingly, abundant metal-oxide units in MOFs possess similar function to MOAC. Therefore, many MOFs are inclined to bond with phosphate groups, the interaction mechanism of which is similar to that of high-valence metal oxides \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\), etc.), namely hard-soft-acid-base (HSAB) theory. Moreover, the large specific surface area and unique porous structure make MOFs ideal host for phosphopeptide retention. In consequence, a multitude of MOFs with different single metal elements such as Zr, Fe, Sn and Er [90–93] were initially designed for phosphopeptide enrichment. For example, \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was synthesized as stationary phase of capillary columns to realize on-column enrichment of phosphopeptide [90]. Sn-based MOFs with \(m\)-phthalic acid ligands were developed for monophophopeptide analysis [92]. However, for a deeper insight of phosphoproteome in complex biological samples, single-metal-centered 3D MOFs are often unsatisfied. Therefore, novel MOFs with more appropriate structure or composition have gradually appeared in the last five years.
ment. For example, \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was synthesized as stationary phase of capillary columns to realize oncolumn enrichment of phosphopeptide [90]. Sn-based MOFs with \(m\) -phthalic acid ligands were developed for monophophopeptide analysis [92]. However, for a deeper insight of phosphoproteome in complex biological samples, singlemetal-centered 3D MOFs are often unsatisfied. Therefore, novel MOFs with more appropriate structure or composition have gradually appeared in the last five years.
2D nanomaterials are prevalent since the discovery of 2D graphene [94]. Inspired by their merits including facile regulation of structure and large specific surface area, 2D MOFs have been gradually explored for phosphoproteome research [95,96]. For instance, Gu's group [95] developed well-defined 2D Ti-MOF nanosheets with nanoscale thickness, large specific surface area and abundant Ti element. The authors compared the enrichment performance of 2D Ti-MOF nanosheets with other materials including similar 2D Zr-based nanosheets, 3D MIL-125 and 3D \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125. The result showed 2D-Ti-MOF nanosheets possessed the best enrichment performance. In detail, 2D-Ti-MOF nanosheets exhibited satisfying sensitivity (0.1 fmol \(\mu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) \(\alpha/\beta\)-casein digest) and extremely high selectivity (\(\beta\)-casein digest/BSA digest=1/10,000, \(n/n\)) for phosphopeptide. On this basis, the 2D Ti-MOF nanosheets disclosed the endogenous phosphopeptidome difference between type 2 diabetes patients and healthy controls. Moreover, the 2D Ti-MOF nanosheets were applied for comprehensive profile of phosphopeptides in mouse tissues including brain neocortex lysate, spinal cord
lysate and testis lysate, and finally disclosed the peptide sequence difference of three phosphorylation sites. Later, by adopting another high-valence metal of Hf, Gu's group [96] designed 2D Hf-BTB nanosheets for selective enrichment of mono-phosphopeptides. In light of the differences between mono-phosphopeptides and multi-phosphopeptides such as number of hydrophilic sites and phosphate groups, the authors rationally designed the hydrophobicity and adjacent distance of metal clusters in 2D Hf-BTB to promise that each metal cluster could only recognize one monophosphopeptide. As a result, 2D Hf-BTB nanosheets exhibited excellent sensitivity (0.4 fmol \(\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) \(\upbeta\)-casein digest) and selectivity (\(\upbeta\)-casein/BSA=1/1,000, \(n/n\)) for monophosphopeptide. Besides, 2D Hf-BTB revealed that all endogenous monophosphopeptides (DpSGEGDFLAEGGGV, ADpSGEGDFLAEGGGV, DpSGEGDFLAEGGGVR, ADpSGEGDFLAEGGGVR) presented upregulation in human serum with inflammation.
Additionally, researchers noticed the biases of various metals to phosphopeptides, and thereby attempted to construct bimetallic MOFs to improve phosphopeptide coverage [97–99]. Zr, Ti and Fe are most commonly employed metal elements, so their combination is preferred. Our group [97] applied Zr and Ti to synthesize a bimetallic MOF composite (named \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{Zr}-\mathrm{Ti}-\mathrm{MOF}\)). Compared with monometallic MOF (\(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{Zr}-\mathrm{MOF}\) or \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{Ti}-\mathrm{MOF}\)), the bimetallic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{Zr}-\mathrm{Ti}-\mathrm{MOF}\) revealed better sensitivity (0.04 versus 0.4 fmol \(\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\), \(\upbeta\)-casein digest) due to more complete chelation process caused by the synergistic effect from Zr–O and Ti–O centers. Moreover, the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{Zr}-\mathrm{Ti}-\mathrm{MOF}\) displayed great size-exclusion effect (\(\upbeta\)-casein digest/\(\upalpha\)-casein BSA=1/5,000/5,000, \(m/m\)), and unbiased enrichment towards monophosphopeptides and multiphosphopeptides. Finally, 44 phosphopeptides including 34 monophosphopeptides and 10 multiphosphopeptides were finally identified from \(1\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\) human saliva. Likewise, Zhang's group [98,99] synthesized two bimetallic MOFs by layer-by-layer self-assembly of MOF shell on magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) core, which were denoted as \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\left(\mathrm{Zr}-\mathrm{Ti}-\mathrm{PTA}\right)_{15}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}(\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Ti})\), respectively. As expected, the resultant bimetallic MOF composites displayed better enrichment performance than monometallic MOF composite. For example, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\left(\mathrm{Zr}-\mathrm{Ti}-\mathrm{PTA}\right)_{15}\) presented better selectivity (\(\upbeta\)-casein digest/\(\upbeta\)-casein digest=1/2,000, \(n/n\)) than \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\left(\mathrm{Zr}-\mathrm{PTA}\right)_{15}\) or \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\left(\mathrm{Ti}-\mathrm{PTA}\right)_{15}\) (\(\upbeta\)-casein digest/BSA digest=1/1,000, \(n/n\)). Recently, Gu's group [100] developed a mesoporous bimetallic MOF (\(\mathrm{Zr}/\mathrm{Ce}\) mesoMOFs) in virtue of a copolymer template strategy and a Hoffmeister ion. The synergistic effect of the two metals and mesoporous channels promised its great performance in phosphorylation analysis of human serum.
Analogously, IMAC and MOAC also show certain en
Analogously, IMAC and MOAC also show certain enrichment preference for phosphopeptides with different numbers of phosphorylation sites. Consequently, the technologies integrating IMAC and MOAC have been explored to facilitate global phosphoproteome profile [101]. Wu's group [102] prepared a MOF with dual Zr centers (named DZMOF) by immobilizing \(\mathrm{Zr}^{4+}\) onto \(\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), during which phosphate groups were utilized as linkers (Figure 2). Since the Zr-O center preferred monophosphopeptides while \(\mathrm{Zr}^{4+}\) center favored multiphosphopeptides, DZMOF exhibited more satisfying recognition ability for the whole phosphopeptides compared with \(\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MOAC or Zr-IMAC, manifesting the advantage of combination of IMAC and MOAC. In detail, DZMOF displayed outstanding sensitivity (0.07 fmol \(\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) \(\upbeta\)-casein digest), recovery (91.4% \(\pm3.2\%\) for a standard phosphopeptide), selectivity (\(\upbeta\)-casein digest/BSA digest=1/5,000, \(n/n\)), and size-exclusion effect (\(\upbeta\)-casein digest/BSA=1/1,000, \(n/n\)). With \(5\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\) healthy saliva as sample, DZMOF identified 5 mono-phosphorylated peptides and 12 multi-phosphorylated peptides. Similarly, Chen's group [103] designed a novel dual-functionalized MOF composite (denoted as DFMMOF) by replacing \(\mathrm{Zr}^{4+}\) of DZMOF with \(\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}\), which displayed extremely outstanding sensitivity (0.025 fmol \(\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) \(\upbeta\)-casein digest), high selectivity (\(\upbeta\)-casein digest/BSA digest=1/1,000, \(n/n\)), great binding capacity (100 mg g\(^{-1}\) for \(\upbeta\)-casein digest) and recovery (84.8% for a standard phosphopeptide). Moreover, the comparison results of human serum and nonfat milk digest indicated DFMMOF possessed more superior capture ability than PDA@ATP-\(\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), for both monophosphopeptides and multiphosphopeptides. Enlightened by the design of DFMMOF, Lin's group [104] adjusted the original micropore structure of \(\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) to hierarchical pore structure by acid etching to prepare \(\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}\)-functionalized THZr-MOFs. Hierarchically porous (HP) channels not only facilitated the accessibility of phosphopeptides to internal metal-oxide affinity sites, but also increased the exposure sites of MOAC and binding energy between \(\mathrm{Zr}/\mathrm{Ti}\) elements and phosphopeptides. Therefore, THZr-MOFs displayed striking sensitivity (0.05 fmol \(\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) \(\upbeta\)-casein digest) and selectivity (\(\upbeta\)-casein digest/BSA digest=1/5,000, \(n/n\)). After treatment with THZr-MOFs, 1,470 phosphorylation sites on 1,432 phosphopeptides (specificity of 85.3%) corresponding to 761 phosphoproteins were identified from human HeLa cell lysate. Notably, it was the precedent of HP-MOFs for phosphopeptide capture. From the above, \(\mathrm{Zr}^{4+}\) or \(\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}\) can be conveniently substituted by other metal ions to obtain more MOF composites.
It has been reported that the improved hydrophilicity can enhance enrichment efficiency of MOFs for phosphopeptides [105]. Wu's group [106] furtherly modified DZMOF with hydrophilic fructose-1,6-diphosphate to obtain DZMOF-FDP for multiphosphopeptide analysis. It is worth mentioning that the binding energy test indicated that Zr
centers tended to present stronger interaction with FDP than monophosphopeptide, while slightly weaker than multiphosphopeptides. So FDP shielded DZMOF-FDP from monophosphopeptides and could be substituted by multiphosphopeptides due to competition effect. Based on this, 1,871 multi-phosphopeptides, \(70.1\%\) of total phosphopeptides, were identified from HeLa cell lysate by DZMOF-FDP. This result is better than that of DZMOF, corroborating the advantage of FDP modification. Additionally, guanidyl (Gua)-functionalized magnetic bimetallic MOF composite \(\mathrm{(Fe_{3}O_{4}@H f/T i-M O F-G u a)}\) was developed in a step-wise manner [107]. Compared with \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Hf/Ti-MOF}\) , the better enrichment ability of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Hf/Ti-MOF-Gua}\) proved the significance of hydrophilic Gua molecules in phosphopeptide recognition. The sensitivity and selectivity of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\varpi)\) Hf/Ti-MOF-Gua for phosphopeptide enrichment were estimated as \(0.2\ \mathrm{fmol\\upmuL^{-1}}\) ( \(\mathbf{\ddot{a}}\) - \(\upbeta\) -casein digest) and \(1/1/2,000/\) 2,000 ( \(\mathbf{\ddot{a}}\) -casein digest/ \(\upbeta\) -casein digest/ \(\mathbf{\ddot{a}}\) -casein/BSA, \(m/m/\) \(m/m)\) , respectively. Additionally, the recovery of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Hf}/\) Ti-MOF-Gua reached \(85.4\%\) for a standard phosphopeptide, and the loading capacity was as high as \(138.46\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , which is larger than that of each monometallic MOF composite. Finally, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Hf/Ti-MOF-Gua}\) realized the easy identification of 27 and 15 phosphopeptides from nonfat milk digest and human saliva by combining with matrix-assisted laser desorption ionization time-of-flight mass spectrometry (MALDI-TOF MS). Another hydrophilic bimetallic MOF composite named \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{Ce-Zr-}\)
MOF@PA was prepared by Wu's group [108]. \(\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}\) and \(\mathrm{Zr}^{4+}\) are both highly charged ions and possess several coordination sites. Therefore, their combination is expected to improve capture capacity. Besides, the immobilization of hydrophilic PA which contained six phosphate groups could not only protect MOF surface but also reduce non-specific adsorption. In this work, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{Ce-Zr-MOF}@\mathrm{PA}\) exhibited prominent sensitivity (0.1 fmol \(\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) \(\upbeta\) -casein digest) and selectivity ( \(\upbeta\) -casein digest/BSA digest \(=1/800\) , \(n/\) \(n)\) , which were better than \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{Ce-MOF}\) , \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\) \(\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{Zr-MOF}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{Ce-Zr-MOF}\) . Finally, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{Ce-Zr-MOF}@\mathrm{PA}\) captured 4, 15 and 19 phosphopeptides from human serum, saliva and nonfat milk digest, respectively. In a word, the combination of IMAC and MOAC as well as the enhancement of hydrophilicity can improve the enrichment efficiency and total coverage for phosphopeptides. The rough summary of MOFs for phosphopeptide analysis is displayed in Table 2.
## 2.3 Simultaneous enrichment of glycopeptides and phosphopeptides
As indicated above, glycosylation and phosphorylation are two most important PTMs. Intriguingly, a competitive or synergetic relationship has been reported between \(O\) -glycosylation and phosphorylation as they both aim at serine and threonine [109,110]. For instance, alternative glycosylation/ phosphorylation of Thr-58 in c-Myc participates in regulat
ing its myriad functions in cells [111]. Phosphorylation level and site are influenced by \(O\)-glycosylation status in glycosylated phosphoprotein Osteopontin which mediates cell adhesion [112]. More importantly, the dynamic interplay between two PTMs is related to human diseases such as Alzheimer's disease, diabetes and cancers [113]. Therefore, comprehensive analysis of glycosylation and phosphorylation is worth attention and may give a more systematic explanation of disease pathogenesis. However, the low abundance and ionization efficacy of themselves, and strong interference from other biomolecules hinder simultaneous MS analysis of glycopeptides and phosphopeptides, thus requiring effective pre-concentration similar to single glycosylation/phosphorylation analysis [114].
With the innate advantage that MOFs can be constructed by hydrophilic organic ligands and metal centers, many MOF composites have emerged to simultaneously enrich glycopeptides and phosphopeptides. The aforementioned UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) is a representative MOF due to its \(Z\mathbf{r}\) center and hydrophilic ligand. Our group firstly synthesized \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathcal{O}\) PDA@UiO-66-NH2 to realize the goal of "one for two" [115]. \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) revealed remarkable sensitivity (0.2 fmol \(\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) HRP digest and 0.02 fmol \(\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) \(\upbeta\) - casein digest) and great selectivity (HRP digest/BSA=1/50 and \(\upbeta\) -casein digest/BSA=1:500, m/m) towards glycopeptides and phosphopeptides, respectively. With human serum digest as sample, 307 \(N\!\cdot\) -glycopeptides from 121 glycoproteins and 33 phosphopeptides from 16 phosphoproteins were identi
fied, respectively. Later, Liang's group [116] replaced \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) with magnetic graphene (magG) to obtain magG@P- DA@UiO-66-NH2 which displayed similar enrichment performance to \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\). MIL-100(Fe) with Fe center and hydrophilic ligand was also employed to construct \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL-100(Fe)}\) [117]. The suitable pore sizes at 1.88 and 3.86 nm endowed \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL-100(Fe)}\) with great size-exclusion effect (HRP digest/BSA=1/500 and \(\upbeta\)-casein digest/BSA=1:500, m/m) and facilitated the capture of 39 endogenous glycopeptides and 43 endogenous phosphopeptides from human saliva samples, respectively.
Hydrophilic MOFs are also fabricated through post-synthetic modification strategies for simultaneous enrichment. For instance, mMIL-125@Au@L-Cys was prepared by furtherly modifying Ti-MOF with L-Cys (Figure 3) [118]. Due to the improved hydrophilicity, mMIL-125@Au@L-Cys revealed better affinity for glycopeptides than mMIL-125. It was further applied to human crystalline lens digest, and 81 \(N\)-glycopeptides corresponding to 35 glycoproteins and 175 phosphopeptides corresponding to 55 phosphoproteins could be identified in two individual samples. As mentioned above, compared with monometallic MOFs, the synergistic effect of two metals (such as \(Z\mathbf{r}\) and Ti) in bimetallic MOFs can induce stronger adsorption ability towards phosphopeptides and improve phosphopeptide coverage. Depending on this, Pan et al. [119] fabricated a bimetallic MOF, namely \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\left(\mathrm{Zr-Ti-MOF}\right)_{10}-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), by layer-by-layer assembly process. \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\)
\((\mathrm{Zr-Ti-MOF})_{10}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) revealed great sensitivity \((0.1\;\mathrm{fmol}\;\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) \(\mathrm{IgG/\upbeta}\) -casein digest), selectivity (IgG digest \(\mathrm{BSA}{=}1/50\) and \(\upbeta\) -casein digest \(/\mathrm{BSA}{=}1{:}2{,}000\) , \(n/n\) ) and loading capacity (200 and \(80\;\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}\) for \(\mathrm{IgG}\) and \(\upbeta\) -casein digest, respectively). Using an optimized single-step enrichment and sequential elution method, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\left(\mathrm{Zr-Ti-MOF}\right)_{10}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) identified 141 \(N\!\cdot\) -glycopeptides corresponding to 127 glycoproteins and 918 phosphopeptides corresponding to 397 phosphoproteins simultaneously from \(0.5~\mathrm{mg}\) of rat brain tryptic digest.
## 2.4. Glycan enrichment
As a pivotal product of glycosylation, glycome plays a central role in biological systems [120]. Changes in glycoform, glycan expression and glycosylation site easily lead to varied diseases [121,122]. It has been indicated that variations on fucosylated and sialylated glycans exist in most cancers [123]. Therefore, glycan analysis is of great importance. Similar to glycopeptide research, the enrichment is a prerequisite of glycan analysis by MS because of the low content and ionization efficacy [124]. Nowadays several techniques based on hydrophilic interaction liquid chromatography, lectin affinity chromatography, boronate affinity
chromatography, porous graphitized carbon (PGC) chromatography, hydrazide chemistry and so on have been extensively employed for glycan enrichment and achieved great progress [125,126]. Among them, PGC chromatography combining adsorption effect with hydrophobic interaction [127] has been preferred for its distinctive capability in recognizing different types of glycans [128,129]. MOFs contain high content of organic ligands, so they are credible carbon source [130–132]. Generally, MOF-derived carbon material will inherit the merits of MOFs such as large specific area and suitable pore structure, which are beneficial to glycan enrichment [133].
As aforementioned, MOFs of MIL series are typical and widely employed due to comparatively easy synthesis process, large specific surface area and structural stability. MIL-101(Cr), a representative MOF, was calcinated at \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with the protection of nitrogen, and the carbonized MIL-101 (Cr) could be used for \(N\!\cdot\) -glycan capture [134]. The large Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of \(531.88\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and the mesopore of \(3.03\;\mathrm{nm}\) provided the carbonized MIL-101(Cr) with adequate binding sites for glycan capture. As a consequence, the carbonized MIL-101 (Cr) exhibited satisfactory sensitivity \((2\;\mathrm{ng}\;\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) OVA digest), size-exclusion effect (OVA digest \(/\mathrm{OVA/BSA}{=}1/1,500/\) \(1,500,\,m/m/m)\) when dealt with standard sample. When applied to serum \(N\!\cdot\) -glycan analysis, 44 \(N\!\cdot\) -glycans were identified by MALDI-TOF MS. MIL-101(Fe) belongs to the same subclass as MIL-101(Cr). Its advantage is that the Fe-O units in MIL-101(Fe) can be converted to magnetic iron oxide by calcination under the protection of inert gas, thus providing strongly magnetic responsiveness for fast separation. Our group [135] calcinated MIL-101(Fe) in argon atmosphere and obtained magnetic carbon material (FeMPC) for the convenient determination of \(N\!\cdot\) -glycans in urine exosomes from healthy people and patients with gastric cancer. Comparison of relative content of \(N\!\cdot\) -glycans in two groups revealed that the up-regulation of sialylation and down-regulation of fucosylation were related to gastric cancer.
ZIFs are also dependable precursors of carbon material owing to milder synthesis condition (usually at room temperature) compared with other series of MOFs. As a precedent, ZIF-67 was calcinated to obtain magnetic mesoporous carbon material for \(N\!\cdot\) -glycan analysis [136,137]. This magnetic mesoporous carbon material exhibited great sensitivity \((1\;\mathrm{ng}\;\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) OVA digest) and size-exclusion effect (OVA digest/OVA/BSA=1/300/300, \(m/m/\) \(m\) ). Furthermore, it identified 31 and 40 \(N\!\cdot\) -glycans from human serum and mouse brain tissue, respectively. ZIF-8 was subsequently exploited as well [138]. It was immobilized on magG sheets (magG@ZIF-8), and then calcinated to prepare C-magG@ZIF-8 composite. In addition to high sensitivity \((1\;\mathrm{ng}\;\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) OVA digest) and size-exclusion effect (OVA digest/OVA/BSA=1/1,000/1,000, \(m/m/m\) ), C
magG@ZIF-8 performed well in treating serum digest. It was reported that metal residues after calcination will decrease the specific surface area and increase non-specific adsorption. From this aspect, the design of porous metal-free carbon material (PMFC) was investigated. After the optimization of temperature, Li et al. [139] selected to calcinate Zn-MOF at \(900\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to obtain PMFC (Figure 4). To improve the water dispersibility, a slight oxidation was applied for PMFC. The obtained oxidic PMFC (oPMFC) performed better than \(Z\mathrm{nO/C}\) composite which was obtained through low-temperature calcination, indicating higher purity of PGC material possessed greater adsorption ability towards glycans. In addition to great sensitivity \((1\mathrm{~ng~}\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1})\) and size-exclusion effect (OVA digest \(/\mathrm{BSA}{=}1/500\) , \(m/m\) ) for \(N\!\cdot\) -glycan capture, oPMFC finally identified 43 \(N\!\cdot\) -glycans from \(0.2~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) human serum.
## 2.5 Enzyme immobilization
As enzymes are essential in proteomics analysis based on bottom-up strategy, enzyme activity and enzymolysis efficiency are noteworthy. Conventional in-solution enzymolysis is often limited by difficult enzyme separation, low operational stability, poor recovery and inferior reusability [140,141]. Perfectly, enzyme immobilization can avoid above drawbacks. Therefore, researchers have explored diverse matrices for enzyme immobilization. MOFs can serve as ideal scaffold for enzyme immobilization due to strong affinity between MOFs and enzymes [140]. The tunable pore structure and morphology also provide more probability to design MOF-enzyme composites. There have been several reliable strategies for the synthesis of MOF-enzyme composite so far, such as surface attachment, covalent linkage, and biomimetic mineralization [142].
ZIF series, especially ZIF-8 and ZIF-90, are of top-priority for enzyme immobilization on account of mild synthesis condition and high chemical stability [143,144]. Lv's group [145] grew ZIF-8 in the pores of \(N\!\cdot\) -(3-aminopropyl)imidazole-functionalized poly(glycidyl methacrylate-co-ethylene dimethacrylate) monoliths by layer-by-layer self-assembly strategy. The obtained ZIF-8@monolith hybrid integrated the strengths of macroporous polymer monoliths and mesoporous ZIF-8. Specifically, macropores allowed a high permeability to flow and enabled rapid convective mass transfer of substrates. Mesopores provided large surface area for enzyme loading. The \(N\!\cdot\) -(3-aminopropyl)imidazole on the monolith enabled the robust binding of enzyme by covalent linkage. Therefore, the ZIF-8@monolith hybrid held much higher loading capacity for trypsin than pristine monolith support, which led to the fast digestion of cytochrome C (Cyt-C) within \(43\mathrm{~s~}\) , with \(100\%\) peptide sequence coverage. Based on the same strategy as above, Qian's group [146] designed a magnetic MOF-enzyme composite, named
\(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{DOTA-ZIF-90}\) -trypsin. Due to the aldehyde groups of ligands and outstanding surface area of ZIF-90, high amount of trypsin was covalently linked. This \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\varpi)\) DOTA-ZIF-90-trypsin resulted in high amino acid sequence coverage \((80\%)\) , complete digestion and reusability (3 times) for BSA under sonication within \(1\ \mathrm{min}\) . More importantly, compared with conventional in-solution digestion for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) , \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{DOTA-ZIF-90}\) -trypsin could shorten the digestion time to \(20\;\mathrm{min}\) , while increase protein/peptide coverage to \(40\%/67\%\) in handling complicated protein samples from 400 mouse oocytes. Later, considering the synthetic procedure of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{DOTA-ZIF-90}\) was tedious and time-consuming, Lin's group [147] developed a biomimetic mineralization-based one-pot method to obtain catalytic iron oxide@ZIF\(8@\) trypsin. The self-assembly of ZIF-8 and immobilization of trypsin on iron oxide substrate simultaneously took place, during which the trypsin was randomly embedded inside or anchored onto the MOF shell. The enzymatic activity of oxide@ZIF-8@trypsin was 2.6 times that of in-solution trypsin. Besides, the reusable oxide@ZIF-8@trypsin revealed superior digestion efficacy for Cyt-C, HRP and BSA compared with in-solution digestion.
Recently, HP-MOFs have been extensively applied in various fields including enzyme immobilization [148–150]. For instance, microporous ZIF-8 was fabricated in the space-confined mesoporous channels of SBA-15 for acquiring a HP-MOF by ligand vapor-induced crystallization (Figure 5) [151]. The uninterrupted mesoporous tunnels accommodated abundant bulky trypsin molecules for combining with ZIF-8 inside the mesopores. This HP structure facilitated the accessibility of interior active sites of ZIF-8 to trypsin for efficient binding, leading to a high loading capacity of trypsin.
## 3 MOFs in environmental research
In the past decade, large amounts of hazardous chemicals,
including plasticizers, pesticides, PPCPs, organic dyes, and heavy metals, have been produced and discharged into environment [152]. Consequently, effective separation and analysis of these contaminants is urgent considering environment safety and human health. As indicated above, MOF-based adsorption method integrates the advantages of adsorption method (high efficiency, low cost, great convenience, etc.) and unique features of MOFs (large specific surface area, ordered pore channels, certain thermal and chemical stability, etc.). Therefore, it is more attractive compared with conventional techniques based on advanced oxidation, biological treatment, and chemical treatment [153]. In this section, applications of MOFs in the removal of plasticizers, pesticides and water pollutants (PPCPs, organic dyes and heavy metals) will be emphatically introduced.
## 3.1 Plasticizer removal
Plasticizers conduce to improve properties of biopolymers including processability, brittleness, and compatibility [154,155], and they therefore are common polymer material additives in industrial production, especially in plastics industries. Phthalate esters (PAEs) are commonly used plasticizers and easily to be migrated into environment because of their non-chemical bonding (such as van der Waals force and hydrogen-bond) to various polymers. Additionally, the accumulation of PAEs in water and crops can alter the functions of human endocrine system and immune system [156]. Moreover, it has been reported that anti-androgen effect of PAEs would destroy male genital system and increase the incidence of cancers [157]. Therefore, developing sensitive and effective methods for plasticizer separation and analysis is necessary. Unique features have promised MOFs as ideal adsorbents for plasticizer detection and removal.
Yamini et al. [158] fabricated two amide-containing ZnMOFs (TMU-23 and TMU-24) for extraction of three plasticizer compounds, including benzylbutylphthalate, di-2-(ethylhexyl) phthalate and di- \(n\) -butyl phthalate, for gas chromatography (GC) analysis. The amide groups containing \(-\mathrm{NH}\) and \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{O}\) could provide dual interaction sites for guest molecules. Compared with TMU-23, TMU-24 possessed more accessibility of amide groups to plasticizers due to the structure difference. Besides, the introduction of naphthalene groups in TMU-24 enhanced the \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction between the adsorbent and analytes. Therefore, TMU-24 performed better than TMU-23 owing to stronger interactions (hydrogen-bond, \(\pi{-}\pi\) and hydrophobic interaction). TMU-24 was further selected for construction of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\) TMU-24. The \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\) TMU-24 exhibited high pre-concentration factors (189–278), recoveries \((92.0\%{-}102.0\%)\) , linear range \((0.5{-}250\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1})\) , limits of detection (LODs, \(0.2–0.4\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{L}^{-1})\) and reproducibility (intra-day relative standard deviation (RSD): \(3.9\%{-}4.7\%\) ; inter-day RSD: \(5.1\%-\) \(5.9\%\) ), proving the reliability of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\) TMU-24 as adsorbent. In addition, it was well known that the substitution of ligands by small aromatic molecules during the preparation of missing-linker defective MOFs can achieve the purpose of increasing porosity without sacrificing adsorption sites. Qiao's group [159] investigated how the content of missing-linker defects influenced the adsorption capability of water-stable UiO-67 towards dimethyl phthalate. In their work, \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}\) was firstly coordinated with Benzoic acid (BA), and then 4,4-biphenyldicarboxylic acid (BPDC) was introduced to compete with BA. Notably, BA and hydrochloride acid were utilized as defect regulator. Due to the acceleration of nucleation rate caused by hydrochloride acid, the missing-linker defects enhanced accompanying by the increase of specific surface area, along with the addition of BA molecules. The resultant UiO-67–30BA with molar ratio of BA and BPDC at 30/1 revealed the greatest number of defects, largest pore volume and specific surface area, and highest adsorption capacity for dimethyl phthalate. This result indicated that high specific surface area and large pore volume played a significant role in the adsorptive process depending on \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking interaction. Kaskel et al. [160] immobilized UiO-67 on \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) via layer-by-layer method, and applied the resulting UiO-67@SiO2 to act as stationary phase for high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) analysis. Owing to its higher number of theoretical plates per meter \((37,570\;\mathrm{m}^{-1})\) than pristine \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) \((24,389\;\mathrm{m}^{-1})\) and the \(\mathrm{MIL-101(Fe)-NH_{2}@SiO_{2}}\) \((4,674\;\mathrm{m}^{-1})\) , only UiO-67@SiO2- based HPLC can distinctly separate dimethyl phthalate and diethyl phthalate.
Molecularly imprinted polymers (MIPs) possess specific and strong recognition cavities for target analytes owing to the target-template synthesis mechanism [161]. The union of MIPs and MOFs is conducive to obtaining adsorbents with
high selectivity. Mirzajani and co-workers [162] put forward a hollow-fiber liquid membrane-protected solid-phase microextraction (SPME) technique based on monolithic and hollow fiber of MOF-DES/MIPs. The introduction of deep eutectic solvents (DES) enhanced the chemical stability of MOFs, and MIPs acted as selective phase for specific analytes. The composite fiber elaborately combined liquid-phase microextraction and SPME. During the adsorption process, target molecules experienced the transfer from aqueous solution to solvent inside the hollow fiber, then from solvent to functional fiber. Finally, the adsorbed PAEs by fiber were released for GC analysis. The results concerning four PAEs (dimethylphthalate, diethylphthalate, di-isobutylphthalate, and \(n\)-dibutylphthalate) revealed the highly selective extraction performance of MOF-DES/MIPs. This should be attributed to the combination of Zn-MOF, DES and MIPs, leading to high capacity, fast adsorption/desorption, accurate analyte positioning and rapid binding rate towards target. Specifically, LODs, limits of quantification (LOQs), RSDs for intra-day and inter-day precisions were in the ranges of \(0.008{-}0.03~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}\), \(0.028{-}0.12~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}\), \(2.4\%{-}4.7\%\) and \(2.6\%{-}3.4\%\), respectively. These four PAEs in edible soybean oil, yogurt, and bottled mineral water sample were further analyzed, and the recoveries achieved \(97.2\%{-}100\%\). Wan's group [163] also combined a MIP with Fe-MOF-74 (denoted as Fe-MOF-74:MIP) for simultaneous adsorption and catalytic degradation of dimethyl phthalate. In detail, the synergistic effect, which was produced by Fe-MOF-74 and MIP, enhanced the adsorption selectivity and capacity. Meanwhile, the dense unsaturated Fe sites in Fe-MOF-74 catalyzed the persulfate-based advanced oxidation processes for dimethyl phthalate elimination, and the degradation rate reached around \(90\%\) within \(30\;\mathrm{min}\).
Considering the convenience of magnetic separation, MOF-based magnetic solid-phase extraction (MSPE) techniques also have been developed for plasticizer removal recently [164–166]. Based on hydrogen-bond, MOF-74 (Ni) layer was fabricated on magnetic \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) by sample ultrasound-assisted method (Figure 6) [164]. This resulting magnetic MOF-74 (Ni) was coupled with HPLC for the determination of diphenyl phthalate, butyl benzyl phthalate, diamyl phthalate and dicyclohexyl phthalate. Thanks to coordination, intermolecular hydrogen-bond, hydrophobic and host-guest \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking interaction and van der Waals force, efficient adsorption of these four PAEs was accomplished. Magnetic MOF-74 (Ni) revealed good linear ranges \((1.53{-}200~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{L}^{-1})\), low LODs \((0.46{-}2.10~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{L}^{-1})\) and great reproducibility \((\mathrm{RSD}{<}3.7\%)\). Besides, it was successfully applied for the detection of PAEs in Chinese liquor samples and presented the recoveries ranging from \(74.4\%\) to \(104.8\%\). Another magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\) multiwalled carbon nanotubes/ MIL-101(Cr) (dubbed MMWCNTs@MIL-101(Cr)) was subsequently proposed by Ebrahimzadeh's group [165] for
the extraction of dimethyl phthalate, diethyl phthalate and diallyl phthalate prior to HPLC analysis. The obvious benefit lays in less usage of adsorbent and organic solvent as well as simple, rapid and efficient adsorption, which was attributed to high porosity and large specific area. Aside from linear ranges of \(0.1{-}1,500.0~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}\), LODs of \(0.03{-}0.15~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}\), reproducibility of \(\mathrm{RSDs}{<}9.3\%\) for three PAEs, the enrichment factors were 89.9, 126 and 92, respectively. Finally, MMWCNTs@MIL-101(Cr) achieved acceptable recoveries of \(74.0\%{-}97.2\%\) when analyzing two water samples and three cream samples.
Moreover, Wu's group [166] utilized Ni-MOF as carbon source to prepare a magnetic Ni-C microsphere by direct calcination under \(400\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with the protection of nitrogen. Magnetic response, high surface area and micro/meso-porous structure led it to be a rapid, efficient and recyclable adsorbent for PAEs including diethyl(o-)phthalate, diallyo\((o)\)-phthalate, dibutyl(o-)phthalate, and dipropyl phthalate. With the aid of HPLC, LODs of 0.2–0.3 and \(0.02{-}0.03~\mathrm{ng}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\), LOQs of 0.6–0.9 and \(0.06{-}0.09~\mathrm{ng}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\), linear ranges of 1.0–100.0 and \(0.1{-}100.0~\mathrm{ng}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\), RSDs for reproducibility \(<\!7.1\%\) and recoveries of \(90.0\%{-}100.6\%\) were determined for four PAEs in juice and water sample, reflecting the great potential of MOF-derived carbon in plasticizer extraction.
## 3.2 Pesticide removal
Pesticides, as important constituent of agricultural production, provide valuable aids to ever-increasing crop yield. However, due to worldwide unreasonable use and poor decomposition ability, excessive pesticide residues can be seen everywhere [167]. It has been reported pesticide residues can impose detrimental effect on human health, including dermatological, gastrointestinal, neurological, carcinogenic, respiratory, reproductive, and endocrine aspects [168]. Many diseases including cancers, Parkinson's and Alzheimer's disease are associated with the exposure to pesticides, for which oxidative stress caused by pesticides is a significant pathogenesis [169]. Therefore, developing credible pretreatment strategies to realize efficient removal of pesticide residues is extremely urgent to ensure public health [170]. The adsorption of pesticides by nanomaterials is prevalent because of low cost, simple operation, high efficiency and great reusability. Particularly, MOF-based adsorption technique has made great progress in this aspect.
Pristine Zr-MOFs including Zr-BTB and UiO-66-NH\(_2\) have dedicated a lot to the removal of different pesticide residues [171,172]. In detail, Zr-BTB was used as adsorbent for five phenylurea herbicides (fenuron, monuron, diuron, linuron and pencycuron) [171]. The affinity between electron donor (heteroatoms in herbicides) and electron acceptor (unsaturated metal sites in Zr-BTB), combining with \(\pi{-}\pi\)
interaction between herbicides and benzene ring in \( Z\mathbf{r} \)-BTB ligand, promised the great adsorption. As a result, great enrichment factors (90, 128, 148, 204 and 295, respectively), adsorption capacities (1.59, 1.64, 1.29, 1.67 and \( 0.49\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1} \)), LODs \( (0.05{-}0.36\;\mathrm{ng}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1}) \) and recoveries \( (85.19\%{-}99.13\%) \) were obtained. Additionally, five phenylurea herbicides in Den-drobii Officinalis Caulis, Salviae Miltiorrhizae Radix et Rhizomaand Hawthorn samples were effectively enriched by \( Z\mathbf{r} \)-BTB and identified by ultra-high performance liquid chromatography (UPLC) coupled with quadrupole TOF-MS. UiO-66- \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \) was fabricated for the extraction of imidacloprid and thiamethoxam at trace level in fruits, followed by the determination by ultra-high performance liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (UPLC-MS/MS) [172]. It exhibited the maximum monolayer adsorption capacities of 318 and \( 378\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1} \) for two pesticides, and the recoveries of \( 92.39\% \) and \( 94.37\% \), respectively, under the optimal conditions. It was speculated that electrostatic and hydrophobic interaction between two pesticides and UiO-66- \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \) motivated the adsorption process. Besides, many properties including high surface area, suitable size and polarity also contributed to the extraordinary performance.
Moreover, magnetic Zr-MOF-based nanocomposites have been extensively developed for MSPE technique recently. For instance, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{m-SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{PSA}@\mathrm{Zr-MOF}\) was constructed for the extraction of bifenthrin prior to GC-MS analysis [173]. This method displayed low LOD of \(0.5\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\), outstanding average recovery of \(103.2\%\) and remarkable producibility with intra-day and inter-day RSD of
\(2.0\%\) and \(3.5\%\) , respectively. Finally, the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{m-SiO}_{2}/\) \(\mathrm{PSA}@\mathrm{Zr-MOF}\) was further applied for the determination of bifenthrin (estimated as \(1.4{\pm}0.1~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) ) in river water samples. Also, magG \(@\) PDA \(@\) Zr-MOF was used as adsorbent for pesticide residues in tobacco samples for GC-MS analysis [174], exhibiting a wide linear range \((150{-}1,000\;\mathrm{ng}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) , low LODs \((10.78{-}45.45\;\mathrm{ng}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) , good reproducibility \((\mathrm{RSD}{<}12.7\%)\) , satisfactory recoveries \((57.9\%{-}126.3\%)\) , and fast extraction process within \(10\;\mathrm{min}\) . The good performance benefitted from \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction and large amounts of binding sites. Similarly, based on the multiple interactions including \(\pi{-}\pi\) , hydrophobic and hydrogen-bond interaction, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF-808}\) was prepared by facile solvothermal method to effectively extract seven benzoylurea insecticides (BUs) [174]. By combining with HPLC, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF-808}\) presented low LODs \((0.04{-}0.15\;\mathrm{ng}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) , wide linear ranges \((0.15{-}50\;\mathrm{ng}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) and satisfactory recoveries \((84.6\%{-}98.3\%)\) in fast detection of BUs in tea beverages and juice samples.
Zr-MOFs can also be integrated with extra functional moieties to improve extraction efficiency. For instance, amino-functionalized Zr-MOF nanoparticles were uniformly distributed on polyacrylonitrile (PAN) fiber by electrospinning technique. The resulting PAN/amino-Zr-MOF nanofiber film could be applied for thin-film microextraction of chlorpyrifos [176]. Combined with thermal induced desorption method and corona discharge ion mobility spectrometry, this PAN/amino-Zr-MOF nanofiber film showed a linear range of \(2{-}200\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\), LOD of \(0.6\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\), and reproducibility with RSD \(<\!11\%\) . Further, the recoveries were
estimated between \(86\%\) and \(109\%\) in dealing with agricultural wastewater and river water samples. Recently, Chen's group [177] designed a cationic \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{Ne}^{3+}\)-based mixed-matrix membrane (MMM), named cationic MOFMMM, for simultaneous extraction of six phenoxy carboxylic acid (PCA) herbicides from water samples for UPLC-MS/MS identification. Mainly owing to the electrostatic attraction between anionic PCA herbicides and \(\mathrm{NMe}^{3+}\) groups in cationic MOFMMM, the better adsorption performance than that of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) MMM were achieved. Meanwhile, other weaker interactions including \(\pi{-}\pi\) conjugation and cation- \(\pi\) bonding together also made a certain contribution to the great results. Zhao's group [178] fabricated the \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{MPCA}\) composite, which was equipped with good hydrophilia, compression resilience and thermostability, by in-situ nucleation and growth of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) on carbon nanotube aerogels (MPCA). The introduction of MPCA not only enhanced the adsorption capacity for chipton and alachlor, but also reduced the leakage of MOF nanoparticles into environment, complying with the purpose of green chemistry. Specifically, \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{MPCA}\) displayed maximum adsorption capacities of 246.8 and \(232.8\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for chipton and alachlor, respectively, which were better than single MOF. This might be attributed to the synergistic effect between MOFs and MPCA.
Zn-MOFs series is another widely employed type of MOFs in pesticide residue treatment heretofore. Bu's group [179] designed NKU-11 with anionic frameworks for adsorption of small cationic pesticide. The mesoporous cages and uncoordinated oxygen atoms were beneficial to NKU11 to own high adsorption capacities of \(160{\pm}5\) and \(200{\pm}5\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for methyl viologen (MV) and diquat (DQ). Magnetic MOF-5-based MSPE coupled with HPLC technique was developed for the simultaneous extraction and determination of four heterocyclic pesticides (carbendazim, triadimefon, chlorfenapyr and fenpyroximate) in water samples [180]. This method presented prominent linearities for carbendazim and triadimefon \((0.3{-}500.0\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1})\) as well as chlorfenapyr and fenpyroximate \((0.1{-}500.0\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1})\). LODs in the range of \(0.04{-}0.11\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) was also impressive. Moreover, great precision of this method was testified by RSDs of \(2.98\%{-}7.11\%\) and \(3.31\%{-}7.12\%\) for intra-day and inter-day, respectively. Therefore, the magnetic MOF-5 was further applied for extracting four pesticides in two reservoir and Yellow River water samples, showing acceptable recoveries between \(80.20\%\) and \(108.33\%\). Xu's group facilely designed a \(\upbeta\)-cyclodextrin (\(\upbeta\)-CD)-functionalized magnetic Zn-MOF (denoted as M-MOF/\(\upbeta\)-CD) by one-pot reaction [181], which was used to enrich prochloraz and three triazole fungicides in vegetable samples. The whole static adsorption process was affected by two potential mechanisms. One was hydrogen-bond and \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction between GO nanosheets and analytes, and the other was the supramolecular recognition between hydrophobic inner cavity of \(\upbeta\)-CD and analytes. M-MOF/\(\upbeta\)-CD revealed much higher adsorption capacities than pristine MOF. The LODs for four fungicides were determined in the range of \(0.25{-}1.0\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\), and the spiked recoveries in the range from \(74.13\%\) to \(119.83\%\). Safari's group [182] created another magnetic Zn-MOF (TMU-6) nanocomposite (named \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{TGA}@\mathrm{TMU}{-}6\)), which acted as efficient adsorbent for organophosphorus pesticides (phosalone, chlorpyrifos and profenofos) in rice and environmental water samples. There were linearity ranges of 7.5–75, 10–100 and \(10{-}150\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\), and LODs of 0.5, 1 and \(0.5\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) for phosalone, chlorpyrifos, and profenofos in water samples, respectively. The great pre-treatment performance of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{TGA}@\mathrm{TMU}{-}6\) relied on the large specific surface area and porous structure of TMU-6 that provided abundant binding sites and strong driving forces involving \(\pi{-}\pi\), hydrophobic and hydrogen-bond interaction.
cular recognition between hydrophobic inner cavity of \(\upbeta\) -CD and analytes. M-MOF \(\upbeta\) -CD revealed much higher adsorption capacities than pristine MOF. The LODs for four fungicides were determined in the range of \(0.25{-}1.0\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) , and the spiked recoveries in the range from \(74.13\%\) to \(119.83\%\) . Safari's group [182] created another magnetic Zn-MOF (TMU-6) nanocomposite (named \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{TGA}@\mathrm{TMU}{-}6)\) , which acted as efficient adsorbent for organophosphorus pesticides (phosalone, chlorpyrifos and profenofos) in rice and environmental water samples. There were linearity ranges of 7.5–75, 10–100 and \(10{-}150\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) , and LODs of 0.5, 1 and \(0.5\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) for phosalone, chlorpyrifos, and profenofos in water samples, respectively. The great pre-treatment performance of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{TGA}@\mathrm{TMU}{-}6\) relied on the large specific surface area and porous structure of TMU-6 that provided abundant binding sites and strong driving forces involving \(\pi{-}\pi\) , hydrophobic and hydrogen-bond interaction.
MIL series has been applied for pesticide residue removal as well. MIL-100(Fe) was prepared by novel and environmentally friendly water-based heatless recipe, with high conversion and convenience [183]. Owing to the large specific surface area of \(1,893\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), MIL-100(Fe) revealed a monolayer adsorption capacity of \(858.11\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for 2,4-dichlorophenoxyacetic acid pesticide in water. The adsorption process was based on electrostatic attraction between deprotonated pesticide and positively charged MOFs. MIL-101(Cr) was modified onto the surface of magnetic composite to obtain \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\)-GO/MIL-101(Cr) for the extraction of triazine herbicides (Figure 7) [184]. Aside from low LODs \((0.010{-}0.080\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1})\), high recoveries \((83.9\%{-}103.5\%)\) and great reproducibility \((\mathrm{RSD}{<}7.8\%)\), \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\)-GO/MIL-101(Cr) revealed great extraction efficiency for seven triazine herbicides in rice samples.
Applications of other MOFs in this field have been continuously reported in the past years. Jalilian's group [185] coated a polyacrylonitrile/nickel-based MOF nanocomposite (dubbed PAN/Ni-MOF) on a stainless steel wire by one-step electrospinning technique. The PAN/Ni-MOF possessed great thermal and mechanical stability, and the introduction of porous Ni-MOF could well induce multiple interactions such as hydrogen-bond, hydrophobic and \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction for diazinon and chlorpyrifos removal. Therefore, it revealed preeminent extraction efficiency including wide linear ranges \((1.0{-}250\) and \(0.5{-}300\;\mathrm{ng}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\), low LODs \((0.3\) and \(0.2\;\mathrm{ng}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\), great reproducibility \((\mathrm{RSD}{<}10.1\%)\) and high recoveries \((87\%{-}98\%)\). Notably, by taking advantage of thermal desorption instead of conventional hazardous organic elution, this method was environmentally friendly. Zhu's group [186] synthesized a magnetic ultrathin 2D nanocomposite (dubbed \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\)-GO-Ni-MOF) for epoxiconazole separation. Owing to the enlarged surface area of special 2D structure and synergistic effect of each compo
nent, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\)-GO-Ni-MOF exhibited a linear range of \(0.01{-}2.00\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\), LOD of \(0.001\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and LOQ of \(0.004\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\). By combining with HPLC, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\)-GO-Ni-MOF was finally employed for the detection of epoxiconazole pesticide in some fruit and vegetable samples. Moreover, a magnetic \(\mathrm{Cu}\)-MOF-based nanocomposite (denoted as MOF-199/\(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\)) was prepared via in-situ growth at room temperature by Wang's group [187]. Depending on the \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction between benzene ring in MOF-199 and delocalized large \(\pi\) bonds in the insecticide molecules, MOF-199/\(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) was successfully applied for MSPE of six neonicotinoid insecticides in environmental water samples for HPLC-MS/MS analysis. This method revealed great linearities \((R^{2}{>}0.9947)\), LODs \((0.3{-}1.5\ \mathrm{ng}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1})\), reproducibility \((\mathrm{RSD}{<}8.1\%)\) and recoveries \((88.0\%{-}107.0\%)\). It is worthy to mention that the lowest loading amount of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) corresponded to the highest extraction efficiency, reflecting the main role of MOF-199 in the adsorption process. The rough summary of MOFs for pesticide removal is displayed in Table 3.
## 3.3 Water pollutant removal
Water pollution has been one of the most severe environmental problems, which is closely related to people's life and human health. Water pollution is mainly caused by PPCPs, organic dyes and heavy metals [188]. Since the 21th century, the purification of water resource has gained more and more attention. Similar to the separation and analysis of pesticides by MOF-based adsorption, numerous MOFs have been exploited to deal with the dilemma concerning wastewater and achieved great success [189–191].
## 3.3.1 PPCP removal
PPCPs including diverse antibiotics, anti-inflammatory, hormone replacements, antipyretics, antiseptics and analgesics have been widely applied for disease cure [192].
However, due to over-use and inappropriate management, PPCPs have been identified as one of the most frequently detected pollutants in aquatic environment over the world, whose toxicity is adverse to human health [193]. It has been reported that PPCPs may cause endocrine disruptions, thus changing hormonal actions [194,195]. There is no doubt that treatment of PPCPs-containing wastewater is in great need. In the past decade, MOF-based adsorption strategy has been well developed for this issue [196–198]. Compared with traditional strategies such as coagulation-flocculation chlorination, electrochemical oxidation, ozonation, and photocatalytic oxidation, MOF-based adsorption strategy reduces the operation difficulty and improves the efficiency at the same time [199,200].
Taking typical Zr-MOFs for instance firstly, a Zr-based MOF (named PCN-777) was constructed by Liu's group [201] to remove cephalexin from water. Because of the synergistic effect of the electrostatic interaction and coordination effect (Figure 8), PCN-777 displayed a high adsorption capacity of \(442.48\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for cephalexin. Another 3D PCN MOF (PCN-134) was adopted as adsorbent for diclofenac [202]. The introduction of tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin (TCPP) pillar linker improved the stability and porosity of 3D MOFs. More importantly, the adsorption properties of PCN-134 could be controlled by adjusting the content of TCPP. PCN30, which corresponded to the addition of \(30\ \mathrm{mg}\) TTCP during the synthesis of PCN-134, revealed the highest uptake of diclofenac due to the balance between porosity and defects (the maximum adsorption capacity of \(2.04\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\)). The mentionable point was that PCN30 could degrade the diclofenac under visible light and realize in-situ regeneration. Interestingly, Chen's group [203] developed a series of UiO-66 analogues (UiO-66-R) by substituting the H atoms on C-2 of terephthalic acid with other groups including \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), \(-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\), \(-\mathrm{Br}\), \(-\mathrm{CH}_{3}\), \(-\mathrm{OH}\), \(-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) and \(-\mathrm{COOH}\), and made a comprehensive comparison for the removal of 2,4-dichlorophenoxyacetic acid, clofibric acid, and diclofenac sodium in wastewater. Thereinto, the highest UiO-66-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) presented 3–4 times of adsorption capacity than the lowest UiO-66-COOH. The structure-function relationship study indicated that specific surface area and multiple interactions including electrostatic, hydrogen-bond and \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction together decided the adsorption capacity. In addition, reports show that HP-MOFs are attractive due to the introduction of mesopores/macropores, which not only provides accessibility to large-size guest molecules but also facilitates the diffusion and mass transport [204]. Zhao's group [205] developed a HP-MOF (named BA-HP-UiO-66) by acid-etching of Zn-MOFs in bimetal Zn/Zr-MOFs and the subsequent boronic acid modification. The pore size of BA-HP-UiO-66 increased from \(0.85\ \mathrm{nm}\) of original UiO-66-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) to a range of \(2.40{-}3.83\ \mathrm{nm}\), and the large specific surface area reached \(630.91\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\). On ac
count of hierarchical pore size and boronate affinity, BA-HPUiO-66 revealed a saturated adsorption capacity of \(526.31\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for teicoplanin with cis-diol structure, which was better than UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\). Huang's group [206] also designed a novel HP Zr-MOF composite (starch-chitosan-UiO-66-COOH) for sulfanilamide-containing sewage treatment. Briefly, microporous UiO-66-COOH nanoparticles were accommodated into the macroporous channels of starch, during which the chitosan acted as an efficient stabilizer to promise the incorporation of MOF nanoparticles through electrostatic interaction and hydrogen-bond interaction. The starch-chitosan-UiO-66-COOH showed homogeneous monolayer chemisorption process, where high affinity between Zr-O center in UiO-66 and sulfanilamide played a critical role. Besides, the mechanical stability of the starch-chitosan-UiO-66-COOH promised its reusability (4 cycles). Sun's group [207] developed a soft foam-like UiO-66/PDA/ BC composite aerogel, considering the credible support role of bacterial cellulose (BC) with high purity and crystallinity, abundant hydroxyl groups, and 3D interconnected structures with ultrafine nanofibers [208–210]. The uniformly dispersed UiO-66 nanoparticles endowed the aerogel with high specific surface area and abundant active sites, which were beneficial to the adsorption of aspirin and tetracycline hydrochloride. The maximum adsorption capacities for two antibiotics were 141.1 and \(184.3\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively. The removal efficiency reached \(77\%\) and \(75\%\), respectively. The better adsorption capacity of tetracycline hydrochloride was attributed to stronger Zr-N coordination effect in addition to weak electrostatic and \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction. The aerogel was reused for six cycles due to its excellent stability.
Cr-MOFs have also been explored for PPCP removal in recent years. Hydrogen-bond is an important mechanism existing in various adsorption processes, which was mentioned several times in the preceding part. The enhancement of hydrogen-bond interaction via modifying MOFs with hydrogen atom-containing groups is expected to improve the adsorption capacity of MOFs. Starting from this point, Jhung's group [211] selected a typical MIL-101(Cr) for postsynthetic modification with \(-\mathrm{OH}\), \(-(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\), \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and \(-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\). The obtained MIL-101s (MIL-101, MIL-101-OH, MIL-101- \((\mathrm{OH})_{2}\), MIL-101- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and MIL-101- \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)) were used for the adsorptive removal of three PPCPs, including naproxen, ibuprofen and oxybenzone, from aqueous solution. The
higher adsorption capacity of MIL-101-(OH) and MIL-101- \((\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) than MIL-101- \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) proved the importance of hydrogen-bond. Subsequently, they conducted a similar comparison about the adsorption capability for five PPCPs, namely \(p\)-chloro-\(m\)-xylenol, bisphenol-A, triclosan, ketoprofen, and naproxen, among pristine MIL-101(Cr) and functionalized MIL-101(Cr) with \(-\mathrm{OH}\) and \(-(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) [212]. It was also demonstrated that hydrogen-bond dominated the adsorption process, in which MOFs and PPCPs acted as H-donor and H-receptor, respectively. Therefore, MIL-101-(OH), with the most hydrogen atoms was considered as a more competitive adsorbent for PPCP removal, especially for \(p\)-chloro-\(m\)-xylenol and ketoprofen. Jhung's work may enlighten researchers to develop more hydrogen-bond-based MOFs by intentionally incorporating functional groups for effective adsorption. Furthermore, to enhance the separability property of MOFs and reduce their loss in practical applications, Yang's group [213] designed two MOF composite beads, namely MIL-101(Cr)/SA and MIL-101(Cr)/CS, by integrating MIL-101(Cr) with sodium alginate (SA) and chitosan (CS). With benzoic acid, ibuprofen and ketoprofen as test sample, MIL-101(Cr)/CS exhibited higher adsorption capacity than MIL-101(Cr)/SA, pure SA bead and pure CS bead. This probably was ascribed to the common \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction and electrostatic attraction between protonated amine groups/CS center in adsorbents and deprotonated carboxyl groups in adsorbates.
Moreover, Al-MOF A100 could be regenerated for at least four times by washing with acetone. Di's group [216] employed a high stable Zn-MOF, namely \(((\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2})_{3}(\mathrm{L})_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{DMF})_{n}\) where L was 1,3,5-tris((pyridin-4-ylthio)methyl)benzene), for a homogeneous monolayer adsorption of tetracycline (displaying capacity of \(30\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) in sewage through elec
trostatic, \(\pi{-}\pi\) and hydrogen-bond interaction.
MOF-derived carbon material was also applicable. A mesoporous carbon material (CDM6-K1000) was obtained by the pyrolysis of Zn-MOF MAF-6 at \(1{,}000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(6\,\mathrm{h}\) in the presence of activator [217], showing markedly increased specific surface area as well as porosity. CDM6-K1000 was used for the adsorption of acidic ibuprofen, slightly acidic oxybenzone and triclosan, basic diclofenac sodium and ate-nolol. The adsorption mechanism of ibuprofen was deeply investigated, from which it was concluded that van der Waals force, hydrophobic interaction, \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction and high-pH-controlled repulsion together affected the adsorption of ibuprofen on CDM6-K1000.
Recently, magnetic MOF nanocomposites also have played a crucial role in this aspect. A wrinkle-structured magnetic MOF composite \((\mathrm{UiO}{-}66@\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66)\) was constructed for the first time and employed for the removal of salicylic acid through electrostatic and \(\pi{-}\pi\) electron donor-acceptor interaction [218]. Briefly, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanoparticles were uniformly dispersed on UiO-66 to form core-shell structured \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\), on the surface of which followed by the further deposition of UiO-66 layer via a seeded growth strategy. The introduction of this wrinkle structure not only increased the specific surface area of MOFs, but also provided more binding sites for salicylic acid. In addition, lattice defects, which were produced in the secondary growth of UiO-66 due to the protrusions of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\), brought about additional coordination interaction between outside MOF layer and salicylic acid under neutral pH. Thus UiO-66@ \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) \((343.5\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) provided \(42\%\) higher adsorption capacity than pristine UiO-66 \((241.9\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\). Similar results were obtained for another two PPCPs of bisphenol A and carbamazepine with electron-donating groups. Furthermore, UiO-66@ \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) was applied for the removal of salicylic acid in wastewater, displaying \(64.5\%\) efficiency and at least 5 cycles of regeneration ability. Moreover, a magnetic core-shell structured microsphere denoted as \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}100(\mathrm{Fe})\) was synthesized by 30-circle assembly and applied for the removal of diclofenac sodium (a representative PPCP) [219]. The mesoporous structure and electrostatic, \(\pi{-}\pi\) and hydrogen-bond interaction together benefitted the whole adsorption process. Therefore \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}100(\mathrm{Fe})\) revealed a maximum adsorption capacity of \(377.36\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) and maintained high removal efficiency \((80\%)\) after 5 cycles. Another core-shell structured nanocomposite \((\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{\mathrm{-Cys@MIL125}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}})\) was prepared for fluoroquinolone removal [220]. Cys not only improved the dispersibility, but also served as effective binding agent for the assembly of MOF shell. Owing to the intensive \(\pi{-}\pi\) and hydrophobic interaction, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{\mathrm{-Cys}}@\mathrm{MIL125}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) exhibited excellent performance in extracting enrofloxacin, fleroxacin, ofloxacin, ciprofloxacin, and sarafloxacin prior to UPLC analysis. Good linearity
\((0.5{-}1,000\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1})\), low LOQs \((0.1{-}0.5\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1})\) and LODs \((0.05{-}0.2\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1})\) for five fluoroquinolones were obtained. Furthermore, recoveries of \(83.8\%{-}109.4\%\) and \(84.8\%{-}108.8\%\) in the spiked tap water and river samples indicated the prospect of this MOF-based MSPE method.
Apart from the most commonly used adsorption technique for PPCPs removal, there are two most acceptable membrane separation technique, reverse osmosis (RO) and nanofiltration (NF). Compared with RO that needs high-cost installation and running, NF possesses not only higher water permeability but also greater retention and selectivity for solute rejection under lower operation pressure and cost [221]. However, conventional polyamide (PA) NF membrane hardly makes a balance between permeability and selectivity. For this, thin-film nanocomposite (TFN) membranes were explored for NF by incorporating multitudinous nanoparticles into PA membrane to improve permselectivity. Thereinto, MOF-based TFN membrane is a splendid one [222]. Balakrishnan's group [221] created a ZIF-8 based membrane for interception of acetaminophen in water. By interfacial polymerization (IP), PA of top layer was coated onto ZIF-8 interlayer which in-situ grew on polysulfone (PSF) support. The obtained PSF/ZIF8/PA MMM provided better interception for acetaminophen \((55\%)\) than PSF/PA \((46\%)\), demonstrating the importance of ZIF-8 in NF process. Typical NF membranes with negative charges are unable to reject pharmaceutically active compounds (PhACs) with positive charge. To solve this problem, Wang's group [223] incorporated a dually charged ED-MIL-101(Cr) into TFN membrane. Strong positively charged ethylenediamine (ED) molecules inside pore channels and negatively charged carboxyl groups on surface helped realize the co-rejection of positively (terbutaline, atenolol and fluoxetine) and negatively charged PhACs (ketoprofen, diclofenac and bezafibrate) with high rejection rates (mostly \(>90\%\)). Although several MOFs have been utilized for TFN-NF membrane design, there is still lack of knowledge on how MOFs interact with PA membrane and influence composite membrane properties. Therefore, Xie's group [224] conducted research on structure-performance relationship of MOF-based TFN-NF membranes. Three hydrostable MOFs, hydrophilic MIL-53 (Al), hydrophilic \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 and hydrophobic ZIF-8, were employed for NF membrane synthesis via two different methods (blending and preloading IP). MOF-based membranes revealed elevated water permeability and comparative selectivity compared with pristine PA membrane. It was verified that blending method could regulate PA physical properties more than IP method. Meanwhile, stronger interaction was found between MIL-53(Al) and PA comparing with the other two MOFs. The differences in membrane synthesis methods, interaction strength with PA and inherent hydrophilicity of MOFs together decided
permselectivity of MOF-based TFN-NF membranes. Further, all the obtained TFN-NF membranes exhibited better or comparable extraction performance for six PhACs.
## 3.3.2 Organic dye removal
Organic dyes, which are produced in many fields including textile printing and dyeing, plastic, rubber, food, leather and paper industry, have been discharged into water in a large amount. The organic dyes can adsorb sunlight, reduce water diaphaneity, consume oxygen and cause oxygen-poor state of water, thus exerting bad impacts on the growth of aquatic organisms and destroying the balance of aquatic system [225]. More seriously, the bad quality of water resource will be harmful to people since some dyes are toxic and carcinogenic [226]. So it is necessary to clear off organic dyes in water. Among masses of strategies, MOF-based membrane separation and MOF-based adsorption are more popular owing to their simple operation, low cost, high efficiency and regeneration [227,228].
As for MOF-based membrane separation strategy, ZrMOFs get wide applications. As seen in Figure 9, water-stable UiO-66 layer grew on a PDA-modified hydrophilic porous GO-PAN (PGP) support layer with the aid of vacuum filtration for dye separation [229]. The retention of dyes relied on the combination of size-exclusion effect and electrostatic interaction. Thanks to the water stability, porous structure and negative charge of UiO-66, the obtained UiO-66/PGP TFN membrane revealed a pure water permeability of \(31.33 \pm 0.75 \, \mathrm{L} \, \mathrm{m}^{-2} \, \mathrm{h}^{-1} \, \mathrm{bar}^{-1}\) and rejection rates more than \(94\%\) for Congo red (CR), methyl orange (MO), rhodamine B (RB) and methylene blue (MB). UiO-66/PGP TFN also possessed anti-fouling properties and long-time stability, both of which are fundamental and necessary for large-scale application of membrane filtration. Alkordi's group [230] constructed a water-permeable and flexible MMM (dubbed MOF@CA MMM) by utilizing hydrostable UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), cellulose acetate (CA) and \(\mathrm{Ca}(\mathrm{CO}_{3})\) as active adsorbent, polymer matrix and porogen, respectively. The introduction of \(\mathrm{Ca}(\mathrm{CO}_{3})\) not only increased pore channels and pore connectivity, but also led to a homogeneous distribution of MOF agglomerates in the CA matrix, which promised the accessibility of dyes to MOFs. Because of the negative surface charge of CA, MOF@CA MMM differently responded to the diffusion and penetration of differently charged dyes, namely in favor of positively charged MB instead of negatively charged MO. However, the adsorption capacity was predominantly determined by UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\). Homogeneous monolayer adsorption and nonhomogeneous adsorption were obtained for cationic MB and anionic MO, respectively. Additionally, this MMM could be regenerated for 10 cycles by simple washing. Wang's group [231] incorporated ultrahigh-molecular weight polyethylene (PE) with high loading of MOFs (86 wt%) for producing MOF-based MMMs
through thermally induced phase separation-hot pressing (TIPS-HoP) strategy. The obtained MOF PE MMMs, including \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 PE MMM and \(\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Cr})\) PE MMM, possessed flexibility, large surface area, mechanical, thermal and chemical stability. More importantly, the microporous channels and nanosized cavities in the MOFs endowed the MMMs with excellent separation performance for small organic dye molecules such as high selectivity, flux and speed. To be specific, rejections (99%) could be realized with a water flux of \(125.7 \, \mathrm{L} \, \mathrm{m}^{-2} \, \mathrm{h}^{-1} \, \mathrm{bar}^{-1}\) under cross-flow filtration mode. Gong's group [232] developed a Zr-MOF-loaded polyurethane foam (Zr-MOFs-PUF) membrane via a hot-pressing process. Because of electrostatic, hydrogen-bond and Lewis acid-base interaction between MOF-based membrane and various dye molecules, Zr-MOFs-PUF could simultaneously adsorbed RB, MB and CR with neutral, positive and negative charges, respectively. Different responses for pH during the adsorption process were validated for three dyes. The maximum removal efficiencies were 98.80% for RB at pH \(\approx 7\), 97.57% for MB at pH \(\approx 9\), and 87.39% for CR at pH \(\approx 3\).
Other MOFs such as ZIFs and Cu-MOFs have been taken into consideration as well. Zhu et al. [233] developed a novel TFN membrane on a hydrolyzed PAN support by incorporating poly(sodium 4-styrenesulfonate)-modified ZIF-8 into a PA layer via a facile IP process. As expected, the introduction of ZIF-8 increased the physical properties of membranes, mainly referring to surface hydrophilicity and water flux. More importantly, this ZIF-8-based TFN membrane revealed prominent nanofiltration performance for organic dyes since ultrahigh retentions of more than 99% were obtained for reactive black 5 and reactive blue 2. Inspired by biomineralization, Fu's group [234] prepared two electrospun-silk-nanofiber (ESF)@MOF membranes named ESF@ZIF-8 and ESF@ZIF-67. This unique method acquired high loading rates of 36% and 34% for ZIF-8 and ZIF-67, respectively, indicating the advantage of numerous nucleating and binding sites for MOFs on ESFs. The high
coverage of MOFs endowed the hybrid membranes with almost complete removal of organic dyes of RB and malachite green (MG). ESF@ZIF-67 membrane was further applied for the establishment of a filtration system for dye separation via a similar removal rate. Moutloali's group [235] fabricated a series of Cu-MOF-based polyethersulfone (PES) composite (Cu(tpa)/GO/GO/PES) membranes by phase inversion method. The addition of GO and MOF Cu(tpa) improved hydrophilicity, permeation flux and rejection selectivity of membranes, thus leading to a highly increased antifouling properties of nearly \(98\%\), rejection for anionic dyes of CR (\(>\!80\%\)) and MO (\(>\!50\%\)). Zhu and co-workers [236] also developed a Cu-MOF/GO-based composite membrane through filtration-assisted assembly of MOF CuTz-1 and GO on a hydrolyzed PAN support. The as-prepared CuTz-1/GO membrane exhibited a high water permeance of \(40.2\,\mathrm{L}\,\mathrm{m}^{-2}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\,\mathrm{bar}^{-1}\) and long-term nanofiltration stability. It revealed exceedingly selective rejection for CR \((99.4\%)\), MB \((94.9\%)\) and direct red \((98.2\%)\). The biggest highlight was the self-cleaning property of CuTz-1/GO membrane, by which the photocatalysis function of CuTz-1 resulted in effective degradation of adsorbed dyes under visible light, promising its regeneration.
Aside from the MOF-based membrane separation, alone MOF-based adsorption strategy has also been intensively explored in recent years. Typically, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Cr) was investigated for the adsorption of different organic dyes [237]. It was verified that the highest adsorption rate and selectivity were obtained for CR, and the maximum adsorption capacity reached \(1,206\,\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at room temperature. The remarkable performance of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-101(Cr) for CR was attributed to several effects including hydrogen-bond interaction, \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking interaction, electrostatic interaction and size-exclusion effect. Qi's group [238] compared the adsorption ability of MIL-125(Ti) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125(Ti) by using three types of dyes (cationic dye, anionic dye and vat dye). It was concluded from measurement results that the electrostatic interaction, \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking interaction and pore filling all contributed to the extraction process of both Ti-MOFs in aqueous solutions. More importantly, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125(Ti) outperformed MIL-125(Ti) since \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) brought extra hydrogen-bond interaction for dye adsorption. Positively/ negatively charged MOFs can be designed by controlling the balance of charge between metal ions and organic ligands [239]. Charged MOFs are able to act as highly selective adsorbents for oppositely charged analytes according to electrostatic interaction. For the sake of efficient removal of negatively charged organic dyes in water, Liu's group [240] prepared two cationic Ni-MOFs by employing two imidazolium ligands containing positively charged moieties. The two Ni-MOFs revealed great efficiency in removing as well as photo-catalytically decomposing anionic dyes of MO, CR and orange II sodium salt from aqueous media, helping the
treatment of contaminated water. Ren and co-workers [241] conducted an intriguing research on ligand-based structure changes of Ag-MOF. When the fluorine atom-containing ligand was changed to trifluoromethyl group-containing ligand, Ag-MOF dimensionality was increased from 2 to 3. It was reported the electron-withdrawing properties of the latter ligand could facilitate the formation of high-dimensional cationic MOF structures with electron-deficient pores, which promoted the electrostatic interaction-based adsorption process for anionic dyes. Hence the as-prepared 3D Ag-MOFs selectively recognized anionic dyes from a mixture of anionic, cationic and neutral dyes in aqueous solutions. Anionic MOFs are also in need for the removal of cationic dyes. Negatively charged MOF nanoparticles named UiO-66-P were synthesized by post-synthetic modification of UiO-66 with phosphate moieties [242]. Undoubtedly, owing to additional electrostatic interaction, UiO-66-P revealed dramatically improved adsorption performance \((91.1\,\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) for cationic dye MB compared with pristine UiO-66 \((24.5\,\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1})\). In addition, UiO-66 exhibited excellent adsorption capacities of 493.1, 172.5 and \(228.6\,\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for the anionic dyes of CR, MO and acid chrome blue K.
As a matter of fact, many novel MOF composites have been designed by using special techniques or substrates for profound applications. Zhan's group [243] developed a novel Cu-MOF/biocompatible polymer scaffold with the emerging, popular and advanced 3D printing technique. The 3D-printed MOF composite could be easily designed into different well-defined shapes, including square, hexagon, and circle. Hexagonal composite was demonstrated to perform best in adsorbing five organic dyes of MB, RB, MG, methyl violet and auramine O and their mixture. Compared with powdery MOF with poor reusability, the 3D-printed MOF composite could be regenerated for at least 7 times by solely immersing in HCl solution. Intriguingly, by optionally adjusting the size of 3D-printed adsorbent, one of the unique characteristics for 3D printing technique, it will be more potential for large-scale industrial application. Zhao's group [244] synthesized the Ni-MOF/GO composite by ultrasonic wave-assisted ball milling, a facile and non-toxic and environmentally friendly technique route, and investigated its adsorption ability for CR. The adsorption capacity of Ni-MOF/GO for CR was as high as \(2,489\,\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). Notably, a proper dry temperature was beneficial to the adsorption process. The removal of water molecules released more unsaturated \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\), which would bond with \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) in CR based on Lewis acid-base interaction. Duan's group [245] proposed a novel idea of loading MOFs onto corncobs by in-situ growth to obtain biodegradable biosorbents. The carbohydrate-rich porous oxidized corncobs (OCBs) with large specific surface area and numerous functional groups served as reliable supports for Cu-MOF (HKUST-1) and Zn-MOF
(ZIF-8). On the one hand, the supports improved stability and reusability of MOFs. On the other hand, MOFs guaranteed the significant role in the adsorption process for dyes. Concretely speaking, the removal efficiency of MO was enhanced from \(8\%\) to \(55\%\) and \(84\%\) for HKUST-I/OCBs and ZIF-8/OCBs, respectively. Oh's group [246] created advanced MOF hybrids (CMFP/ZIFs) by integrating ZIFs (ZIF-8 or ZIF-67) with flexible carboxymethylated filter paper (CMFP). Negatively charged \(\mathrm{MO}^{-}\)was rapidly and effectively removed after a simple filtration manipulation owing to the positive surface charge of ZIFs. The MOF papers could be reused by washing with methanol.
## 3.3.3 Heavy metal removal
Heavy metals (As, Pb, Hg, Cd, etc.) with high toxicity produced from various industries have extensively entered into the global aquatic system and caused environment crisis. Heavy metals are able to interact with proteins in human bodies, leading to their inactivation and causing life-threatening injuries [247]. The bioaccumulation of non-degradable heavy metals in bodies also contributes to chronic intoxication of several organs including brain, liver and kidney [153,248]. The treatment of heavy metals has always been an urgent issue in the past decades. MOF-based adsorption and membrane separation stand out in this research field compared with other strategies such as chemical precipitation, electrochemical technique, ion-exchange and biological treatment [249–251].
Zr-MOF-based adsorption has often been considered in the first place [252]. In Luo's work [253], UiO-66 was developed for the removal of As, in which the bonds of \(\mathrm{Zr-O}\) played a significant role in the formation of bidentate mononuclear complexes and bidentate binuclear complexes for As(V) and As(III), respectively. To be specific, UiO-66 not only possessed maximum adsorption capacities of 205 and \(68.21\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for As(III) and As(V), respectively, but also maintained the exceptional adsorption ability in relatively wide \(\mathrm{pH}\) ranges. Besides, the great adsorption performance of UiO-66 decreased a little after six regeneration tests, indicating its stability and reusability. Yin's group [254] developed a melamine-modified \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) in one step. Based on the coordination interaction between \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\), this MOF composite could achieve the highest adsorption capacity of \(205\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(\mathrm{pH}\;6\). Besides, a thiophene containing Zr-MOF named DUT-67 was designed for \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) removal as well [255]. Putative \(6\pi-\eta^{5}\)-thiophene-\(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) coordination for efficacious recognition of \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) by DUT-67 was proved by solution-phase \({}^{207}\mathrm{Pb}\) nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy. Based on the above, DUT-67 revealed a maximum loading capacity of \(98.5\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), equal to two \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) ions accommodated within a pore. Recently, Wang's group [256] uniformly immobilized mercaptan-containing Zr-DMBD MOF nanoparticles on the surface of
3D kenaf stem-derived carbon (KSC) to obtain a dual-functional novel composite (Zr-DMBD MOFs/3D-KSC) for simultaneous detection and removal of \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\). The macroporous structure of 3D-KSC accelerated the mass transfer and a great deal of mercaptan groups on the pore surface enhanced the retention of \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\). Accordingly, the composite not only showed high sensitivity, selectivity and reproducibility for \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\) detection, but also effectively decreased the concentration of \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\) in real wastewater to be less than \(0.05\;\mathrm{ppm}\), a national discharge standard of industrial wastewater (GB30770–2014). Defects often exist in MOF structures and can be divided into three classes, namely inherent defects, missing linker defects and missing cluster defects. These defects generally influence the stability, reactivity and porosity of MOFs [257–259] that are closely related to their adsorption ability [260,261]. In light of this, Wang's group [262] prepared eight \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\) MOFs with three different defects (MOF-808 and NU-1000; UiO-66-Ac, UiO-66-HCl and UiO-67-HCl; UiO-66–36Dif, UiO-66–12Trif and UiO-66–36Trif) and compared their adsorption capacities for \(\mathrm{Se}(\mathrm{VI})\) oxyanions. It was found the adsorption was based on the anion-exchange between oxyanions and ligands, and the more efficient and faster uptake of oxyanions can be realized by higher missing linker/cluster defect densities. Besides, the better adsorption capacity of UiO-66-HCl \((86.8\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) than NU-1000 \((78.8\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) proved the value of modulator synthetic strategy in designing defective MOFs for better adsorption applications.
Zr-MOFs have also been utilized for membrane separation of heavy metal ions. UiO-66-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), which was synthesized by microwave-assisted method within only \(3\;\mathrm{min}\), was combined with ceramic membrane ultrafiltration for the removal of \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) in wastewater [263]. The high removal efficiency \((61.4\%)\) and adsorption capacity \((1,795.3\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) were obtained owing to the coordination of \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) in MOFs with \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) as indicated above. The \(\mathrm{pH}\)-controlled adsorption process made \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) desorption easier, thus favoring the reuse of MOFs. Zr-MOF-808 and Fe-MOF-8300 were integrated with water-stable PAN and polyvinylidene fluoride for preparing nanofibrous MOF membranes (NMOM) by electrospinning technique for heavy metal removal for the first time [264]. It was found that pristine MOF-300 performed better than MOF-808, and hydrophilicity of PAN was conducive to adsorption. So MOF-300-based PAN nanofibers (PA 300) exhibited best adsorption performance for \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) removal in \(395\;\mathrm{mL}\) of \(100\;\mathrm{ppb}\;\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) solution with a high flux of \(348\;\mathrm{L}\;\mathrm{m}^{-2}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) and a permeance of \(870\;\mathrm{L}\;\mathrm{m}^{-1}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{bar}^{-1}\). Three potential mechanisms including competitive ion exchange, electrostatic interaction and binding to open metal sites in pores were reported to be related to the adsorption process. Zr-MOF-88-based NMOM named PAN/MOF-808 was also employed by Efome's group [265] for the removal of \(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{II})\) and \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{II})\). The maximum adsorption capacities for two
metal ions were 225.05 and \(287.06\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively. Moreover, the influence of different activation methods on adsorption ability was investigated. With a flux of \(348\;\mathrm{L}\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\), PAN/MOF-808 experiencing hydroactivation could treat \(580\;\mathrm{mL}\) of Cd solution, which was more than that of conventional vacuum activation-treated composite membrane \((464\;\mathrm{mL})\). The better performance of hydroactivation was attributed to the enlargement of pore volume. That is to say, adsorption sites were increased in each pore volume. In addition to the applications in composite membrane, microporous Zr-MOF-808 was filled within a macroporous chitin matrix to establish a HP composite [266], which acted as a multi-functional adsorbent for a wide scope of pollutants. Synergistic effect, which resulted from the compartmentalization of chitin macropores by accommodating MOF-808 nanoparticles and active interface between two individual components, contributed to the efficient chemisorption of As(V) based on covalent bonding of Zr-O-As bridges.
Applications of other MOFs for adsorptive removal of heavy metals are also worth mentioning. As displayed in Figure 10, Wang's group [267] utilized MIL-101-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{Fe})\), which was synthesized in one-step manner, for simultaneous sensing and removal of Fe(III), \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\). The saturated capacities for three heavy metals were 3.5, 0.9, \(1.1\;\mathrm{mM}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). This dual-function was correlated to the introduction of \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) in MOFs, so other four amino-containing MOFs including MIL-101-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{Cr})\), MIL-53-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{Al})\), \(\mathrm{UiO}-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{Zr})\) and \(\mathrm{MOF}-5-\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{Zn})\) could also realize the same goal. Bu's group [268] developed a hydrostable luminescent MOF named NUM-5 for highly sensitive and selective detection of \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) \(\left(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}\right.\) and \(\left.\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}\right)\). Based on luminescence quenching induced by competitive adsorption and resonance energy transfer between \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) and NUM-5, this MOF revealed extremely low LODs of 0.7 and \(0.3\;\mathrm{ppm}\) for \(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}\) and \(\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}\), respectively. Urea-functionalized \(\mathrm{Zn}-\mathrm{MOFs}\), TMU-31 and TMU-32, were developed by Morsali's group via rapid, green, convenient, solventless and low-cost mechanosynthesis [269]. Coordination effect between heavy metal ions and abundant urea groups led to the retention of \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) inside the pore channels. Therefore, maximum absorption capacities of 476.19 and \(909\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) within \(15\;\mathrm{min}\) were obtained by TMU-31, while 417 and \(909\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) by TMU-32. Moreover, a dual-functional Co-based MOF (TMU-81) was constructed by incorporating sulfonyl and amide groups for heavy metal ion removal [270]. On the one hand, a maximum adsorption capacity of \(526\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) could be achieved for \(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{II})\). The negatively charged pore surface caused by the immobilized oxygen-containing groups and size-exclusion effect based on relatively small pore size resulted in the selective adsorption of small-size \(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{II})\) from the simulated wastewater containing various metal ions. On the other hand,
metal-containing TMU-81 could not only be reused for at least 5 cycles after easy washing, but also be converted to metal-carbon hybrids by pyrolysis at \(800\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under argon atmosphere for other applications. Polysulfides can bond with soft heavy metal ions by soft-soft interaction but lack water stability. From this point, Yamini and co-workers [271] skillfully designed a \(\mathrm{S}_{4}-\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{MOF}\) via coordination between polysulfides and unsaturated \(\mathrm{Cu}\) center in \(\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{MOF}\). In such MOF composites, MOFs protected polysulfides in aqueous system, in turn, polysulfides provided MOFs with high extraction efficiency for heavy metals. Undoubtedly, \(\mathrm{S}_{4}-\mathrm{MOF}\) exhibited extraction recovery of \(100\%\) and \(0.13\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\). In the meanwhile, \(\mathrm{S}_{4}-\mathrm{MOF}\) revealed high selectivity for different metal ions in the order of \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})>\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})>\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{II})>\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{II})>\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\), owing to the different \(\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{S}\) bonding strength. The rough summary of MOFs for heavy metal removal is displayed in Table 4.
## 4 Conclusions and perspective
In this review, we make a brief summary of recent developments in MOFs as advanced adsorbents for sample separation and analysis. Advantages of large specific surface area, tunable porosity and ordered porous channels, structure diversities, thermal and chemical stability provide a credible basis for the selection of MOFs as promising adsorbents among various nanomaterials. Unique functionalities derived from metal centers or organic ligands further contribute to their prominent adsorption performance in proteomics and environmental research. For instance, the recognition of phosphopeptides depends on the metal center-based MOAC technique, and the adsorption of plasticizers/pesticides is based on the hydrophobic and \(\pi-\pi\) stacking interaction between organic ligands and analytes. Therefore, by rationally combining metal ions and organic linkers, researchers can facilely design MOFs to meet varied requirements. Besides, strategies of alternation in MOFs structures (defective MOFs and HP-MOFs) and integrating MOFs with other functional composites \(\left(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOFs}, \mathrm{N MOM}, \text{etc.}\right)\) have got con
tinuous development, since the extra functional composites are bound to endow MOFs with new properties, which is beneficial to improve sample separation efficiency.
Undoubtedly, remarkable progress has been made in MOFs-based sample pre-treatment, but several problems and challenges exist and need to be overcome. (1) Many MOFs are inclined to decompose in water and acid/base aqueous solution, which hinders their practical applications to a large extent. Especially, water stability is the premise for the employment of MOFs for water contaminant removal. Accordingly, improvement in MOFs stability is an extremely important aspect in future research. (2) The synthetic routes for some MOFs are complicated and time-consuming, and sometimes the raw materials for MOFs are expensive, which hinder their large-scale applications. Reducing the synthesis complexity of existing MOFs or developing new functional MOFs in facile and time-saving manners as well as comparatively low cost is alternative. (3) The constituents of some MOFs are toxic and may cause new pollution in the environment if treated improperly. Therefore, the regeneration and reusability are still worth attention in future design of MOFs and MOF composites, which conforms to the purpose of green chemistry. (4) Structure-function relationship has been proved in MOFs with defects or hierarchical pore structure, but still lacks applications. More reasonable MOF structures had better be taken into consideration to enhance the extraction performance. (5) The adsorption mechanisms of MOFs for some analytes are still unclear, restraining their further practical applications. It is expected that more explorations can be conducted to help disclose the underlying mechanisms, thus broadening the application scope.
Although the road to explore and expand MOF applications is long, troublesome and even endless, researchers will keep devoting themselves to their passionate career. As the saying goes, "The way ahead is long, I shall search high and low".
